// --- Lesson 1: Why Europeans Explored and the Portuguese Voyages ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Why Did Europeans Want to Explore?\n\nIn the **15th century** (1400s), European countries began sending ships to explore unknown parts of the world. Several reasons drove this **Age of Exploration**.\n\n### Reasons for European Exploration\n\n| Reason | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Trade** | Europeans wanted to find a **sea route to Asia** to buy spices, silk, and other luxury goods directly, instead of paying high prices to middlemen |\n| **Religion** | Europeans wanted to spread **Christianity** to new lands |\n| **Wealth** | Explorers hoped to find **gold, silver**, and other riches |\n| **Competition** | European kingdoms competed with each other for power, land, and trade routes |\n| **Curiosity** | New technology (better ships, compasses, maps) made long voyages possible and fuelled a spirit of adventure |\n\n### New Technology Made Exploration Possible\n\n| Technology | How It Helped |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| **Compass** | Showed direction even when land was out of sight |\n| **Astrolabe** | Helped sailors determine their latitude using the position of stars |\n| **Caravel** | A small, fast ship that could sail against the wind |\n| **Better maps** | More accurate maps of coastlines and ocean currents |\n| **Gunpowder and cannons** | Gave Europeans military advantage over peoples they encountered |'),
  q(2, 'What was the main reason Europeans wanted to find a sea route to Asia?',
    ['To buy spices and luxury goods directly without paying high prices to middlemen', 'To find a shorter route to America', 'To escape religious persecution in Europe', 'To find new places to grow crops'], 0,
    'Spices like pepper, cinnamon, and cloves were extremely valuable in Europe for flavouring and preserving food. The overland trade route through the Middle East was controlled by Muslim traders who charged high prices. A sea route would allow Europeans to buy directly from Asia.'),
  t(3, '## Portuguese Explorers — Dias and Da Gama\n\n**Portugal** was the first European country to send explorers down the coast of Africa. Prince **Henry the Navigator** established a school of navigation and sponsored voyages of exploration.\n\n### Bartolomeu Dias (1488)\n\n| Detail | Information |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Goal** | To find a sea route around Africa to Asia |\n| **Achievement** | First European to sail around the **southern tip of Africa** |\n| **Cape of Storms** | Dias named the southern tip the "Cape of Storms" because of the terrible weather |\n| **Renamed** | King John II of Portugal renamed it the **Cape of Good Hope** — hope of finding the route to India |\n| **Turned back** | Dias\'s crew was exhausted and forced him to turn back |\n\n### Vasco da Gama (1497–1498)\n\n| Detail | Information |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Goal** | To complete the route to India by sea |\n| **Achievement** | First European to sail from Europe to **India** around Africa |\n| **Route** | Sailed from Lisbon, around the Cape, up the east coast of Africa, then across the Indian Ocean to Calicut (India) |\n| **Help** | Used the knowledge of an **Arab navigator** (Ahmad ibn Majid) to cross the Indian Ocean |\n| **Impact** | Opened the **spice trade** route and made Portugal wealthy |\n\nDa Gama\'s voyage proved that it was possible to reach Asia by sea, changing world trade forever.'),
  fb(4, 'Bartolomeu ___ was the first European to sail around the southern tip of Africa in 1488. Vasco da Gama completed the sea route to ___ in 1498.',
    ['Dias', 'India'],
    'Dias reached the Cape in 1488 but was forced to turn back by his tired crew. Ten years later, Vasco da Gama completed the journey all the way to India, opening a sea trade route that made Portugal one of the wealthiest nations in Europe.'),
  t(5, '## The Impact of Exploration on Indigenous Peoples\n\nEuropean exploration had **devastating consequences** for the indigenous peoples of Africa, the Americas, and Asia.\n\n### Negative Effects on Indigenous Peoples\n\n| Effect | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Loss of land** | Europeans claimed land that already belonged to indigenous peoples |\n| **Violence** | Europeans used **guns and cannons** against people armed with spears and bows |\n| **Disease** | Europeans brought diseases like **smallpox** and **measles** that killed millions of indigenous people who had no immunity |\n| **Slavery** | Millions of people were **enslaved** and forced to work for Europeans |\n| **Loss of culture** | Europeans tried to replace indigenous languages, religions, and customs with their own |\n| **Exploitation of resources** | Europeans took gold, silver, spices, and other resources from the lands they conquered |\n\n### The Khoikhoi and San of Southern Africa\n\n- The **Khoikhoi** (herders) and **San** (hunter-gatherers) were the original inhabitants of southern Africa\n- They had lived in the region for **thousands of years**\n- When Europeans arrived, the Khoikhoi and San lost their land, livestock, and way of life\n- Many were killed, enslaved, or forced to work for the settlers'),
  q(6, 'Which of the following was a consequence of European exploration for indigenous peoples?',
    ['Loss of land, exposure to new deadly diseases, enslavement, and destruction of their cultures', 'Indigenous peoples became wealthier through trade', 'Europeans shared their technology equally with indigenous peoples', 'There were no negative effects'], 0,
    'European exploration had devastating effects on indigenous peoples around the world. They lost their land to settlers, died from diseases they had never encountered before, were enslaved, and saw their cultures suppressed. These effects are still felt today.'),
  t(7, '## Jan van Riebeeck and the Dutch at the Cape\n\nIn **1652**, the Dutch East India Company (VOC) sent **Jan van Riebeeck** to set up a **refreshment station** at the Cape of Good Hope.\n\n### Why Did the Dutch Come to the Cape?\n\n- The VOC traded between the Netherlands and Asia (the **East Indies**)\n- Ships needed to stop halfway to get **fresh water, food, and firewood**\n- The Cape was a good location — roughly halfway between Europe and Asia\n- Van Riebeeck\'s job was to grow vegetables, keep animals, and trade with the **Khoikhoi** for cattle and sheep\n\n### The Refreshment Station\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Arrived** | 6 April 1652 |\n| **Location** | Table Bay (present-day Cape Town) |\n| **Purpose** | Provide fresh food and water for VOC ships |\n| **Garden** | Van Riebeeck planted the **Company\'s Garden** (it still exists in central Cape Town today) |\n| **Fort** | A small fort was built for protection |\n| **Workers** | VOC employees and later **enslaved people** from Southeast Asia and East Africa |\n\n### Relations with the Khoikhoi\n\n- At first, the Khoikhoi traded **cattle and sheep** willingly with the Dutch\n- As more settlers arrived and took more land, the Khoikhoi began to resist\n- The Dutch **fenced off land** with a bitter almond hedge, keeping the Khoikhoi out\n- Wars broke out (the **Khoikhoi-Dutch Wars** of 1659 and 1673)\n- The Khoikhoi lost their land and many were forced to work on Dutch farms'),
  fb(8, 'Jan van Riebeeck arrived at the Cape on 6 April ___ to set up a refreshment station for the Dutch East India Company. The Khoikhoi lost their land as more Dutch ___ arrived and took over their grazing areas.',
    ['1652', 'settlers'],
    'Van Riebeeck\'s arrival in 1652 marks the beginning of European settlement at the Cape. What started as a small refreshment station grew into a colony. The Khoikhoi, who had lived at the Cape for thousands of years, gradually lost their land and independence to the expanding Dutch settlement.'),
  q(9, 'What was the original purpose of the Dutch settlement at the Cape?',
    ['To serve as a refreshment station providing fresh food and water for VOC ships travelling between Europe and Asia', 'To start a colony and conquer Southern Africa', 'To mine gold and diamonds', 'To convert the Khoikhoi to Christianity'], 0,
    'The VOC (Dutch East India Company) did not originally plan to create a colony. They wanted a small station where ships could stop for fresh vegetables, water, meat, and firewood on the long voyage between the Netherlands and Asia. However, the settlement gradually expanded beyond its original purpose.'),
  t(10, '## The Significance of European Exploration\n\n### Different Perspectives\n\nEuropean exploration looks very different depending on whose perspective you take:\n\n| European Perspective | Indigenous Perspective |\n|---------------------|----------------------|\n| Discovery of new lands | Invasion of their homeland |\n| Spread of Christianity | Destruction of their religions |\n| Bringing "civilisation" | Destruction of existing cultures |\n| Opening trade routes | Exploitation and slavery |\n| Building new settlements | Theft of their land |\n\n### Lessons for Today\n\n- It is important to look at history from **multiple perspectives**, not just the European one\n- The effects of colonisation are still felt today in **poverty, inequality**, and **land disputes**\n- Understanding this history helps us work towards **justice and reconciliation** in modern South Africa\n- We must recognise and respect the **rights of indigenous peoples** who lived on the land long before European settlers arrived\n\nIn South Africa, this history connects directly to the struggle for democracy and human rights that we studied in Chapter 1. The land dispossession that began in 1652 was a root cause of the inequality that apartheid deepened — and that our democracy is still working to overcome.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: The Dutch Settlement and Its Impact ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Life at the Cape Settlement\n\nAfter Jan van Riebeeck arrived in 1652, the Dutch settlement at the Cape slowly grew from a small refreshment station into a **colony**.\n\n### Free Burghers\n\n- In **1657**, the VOC released some of its employees from their contracts and gave them land to farm\n- These people were called **free burghers** (free citizens)\n- They were supposed to grow food to sell to the VOC at fixed prices\n- The free burghers expanded the settlement beyond the original refreshment station\n- They took **more and more Khoikhoi land** for farming\n\n### Enslaved People at the Cape\n\n- The VOC brought **enslaved people** to the Cape from **1658** onward\n- Enslaved people came from **Madagascar, Mozambique, India, Sri Lanka, Malaysia**, and **Indonesia**\n- By 1700, there were more enslaved people at the Cape than free settlers\n- Enslaved people did most of the **hard labour** — farming, building, cooking, and domestic work\n- They were treated as **property** and had no rights\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **First slaves arrived** | 1658 (from Angola and West Africa) |\n| **Origins** | East Africa, Madagascar, India, Southeast Asia |\n| **Work** | Farming, construction, domestic service, skilled trades |\n| **Treatment** | Harsh punishment, no freedom, families separated |\n| **Abolished** | Slavery was abolished at the Cape in **1834** |'),
  q(2, 'Where did the enslaved people at the Cape come from?',
    ['Various places including Madagascar, Mozambique, India, Sri Lanka, Malaysia, and Indonesia', 'Only from West Africa', 'Only from Europe', 'They were all local Khoikhoi people'], 0,
    'Cape slavery was unique because enslaved people came from a wide range of places across the Indian Ocean world. This diversity of origins — from Africa, South Asia, and Southeast Asia — contributed to the rich cultural mix that shaped Cape Town and the development of Afrikaans.'),
  t(3, '## The Khoikhoi — A People Dispossessed\n\nThe **Khoikhoi** were herders who had lived in the Cape region for thousands of years before the Dutch arrived.\n\n### Khoikhoi Way of Life\n\n- The Khoikhoi were **pastoralists** — they herded cattle, sheep, and goats\n- They moved with their livestock to find fresh **grazing land** (semi-nomadic)\n- They lived in **portable dome-shaped huts** made from reed mats\n- Their society was organised into **clans** led by **chiefs**\n- Cattle were a sign of **wealth and status**\n\n### What Happened to the Khoikhoi\n\n| Period | Events |\n|--------|--------|\n| **1652–1657** | Khoikhoi traded cattle and sheep with the Dutch |\n| **1657 onwards** | Free burghers took Khoikhoi land for farming |\n| **1659** | First **Khoikhoi-Dutch War** — Khoikhoi tried to reclaim their land |\n| **1673** | Second Khoikhoi-Dutch War — Khoikhoi defeated |\n| **1713** | **Smallpox epidemic** killed large numbers of Khoikhoi who had no immunity to the disease |\n| **After 1713** | Many surviving Khoikhoi were forced to work on Dutch farms as labourers |\n\n### A Khoikhoi Leader\'s Words\n\nThe Khoikhoi leader **Doman** (also called Harry) said to the Dutch:\n> *"Who should rather go away, the rightful owner of the land or the foreign invader?"*\n\nThis quote shows that the Khoikhoi understood what was happening — their land was being stolen.'),
  fb(4, 'The Khoikhoi were ___ who herded cattle, sheep, and goats. A devastating ___ epidemic in 1713 killed large numbers of Khoikhoi who had no immunity to the disease.',
    ['pastoralists', 'smallpox'],
    'The Khoikhoi were pastoralists — people whose way of life centred on herding animals. The smallpox epidemic of 1713 was catastrophic because the Khoikhoi had never been exposed to the disease and had no natural immunity. It destroyed entire communities.'),
  t(5, '## The San — Hunter-Gatherers of Southern Africa\n\nThe **San** (sometimes called Bushmen) were **hunter-gatherers** who had lived in southern Africa for tens of thousands of years — they are among the **oldest peoples on Earth**.\n\n### San Way of Life\n\n| Aspect | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Food** | Hunted animals with **poisoned arrows** and gathered wild plants, roots, and berries |\n| **Shelter** | Lived in temporary shelters made from branches and grass |\n| **Society** | Lived in small, egalitarian bands of 20–80 people — no chiefs or kings |\n| **Art** | Created **rock art** (paintings and engravings) found across southern Africa — some over 20 000 years old |\n| **Knowledge** | Had deep knowledge of the natural environment — plants, animals, water sources |\n| **Language** | Spoke languages with distinctive **click sounds** |\n\n### What Happened to the San\n\n- The San clashed with both **Khoikhoi herders** and **European settlers** over land\n- Dutch settlers hunted San people in organised **commandos** (armed raiding parties)\n- The San were pushed into increasingly **remote areas** (the Kalahari Desert)\n- Many were killed, and their way of life was largely destroyed\n- Today, San descendants live in Botswana, Namibia, and South Africa, and there are efforts to preserve their culture and languages'),
  q(6, 'What is historically significant about San rock art?',
    ['It is among the oldest art in the world, with some paintings over 20 000 years old, showing the San\'s deep connection to their environment', 'It was painted by European settlers', 'It was created less than 100 years ago', 'It only shows pictures of European ships'], 0,
    'San rock art is found across southern Africa and includes some of the oldest artworks in the world. The paintings and engravings depict animals, hunting scenes, dances, and spiritual experiences, providing a remarkable window into the lives and beliefs of the San over thousands of years.'),
  t(7, '## The Growth of the Cape Colony\n\nBy the early 1700s, the Cape settlement had grown far beyond its original purpose as a refreshment station.\n\n### How the Colony Expanded\n\n| Development | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| **Free burghers** | Settlers moved further from Cape Town to farm |\n| **French Huguenots (1688)** | French Protestants fleeing religious persecution settled in **Franschhoek** and **Stellenbosch**, planting vineyards |\n| **Trekboers** | Settlers who moved deep into the interior with their livestock, far from VOC control |\n| **New towns** | Stellenbosch (1679), Paarl, and other settlements were established |\n\n### The Development of Afrikaans\n\n- **Afrikaans** developed at the Cape as a new language\n- It grew from a mix of **Dutch**, **Malay**, **Portuguese**, **Khoikhoi**, and other languages\n- It was spoken by settlers, enslaved people, and Khoikhoi workers\n- It started as a spoken language (called *Kombuistaal* — kitchen language) before becoming a written language much later\n\n### The Cape\'s Cultural Mix\n\nThe Cape Colony brought together people from many backgrounds:\n- **Dutch, German, and French** settlers\n- **Khoikhoi and San** indigenous peoples\n- **Enslaved people** from Africa, India, and Southeast Asia\n\nThis mixing of cultures created the unique heritage of the Cape — its food (bobotie, koeksisters), music (ghoema), and languages.'),
  fb(8, 'French ___ arrived at the Cape in 1688 and settled in areas like Franschhoek and Stellenbosch. The Afrikaans language developed from a mix of Dutch, Malay, Portuguese, and ___ languages.',
    ['Huguenots', 'Khoikhoi'],
    'The French Huguenots were Protestants who fled religious persecution in France. They brought winemaking skills to the Cape, helping to establish the wine industry. Afrikaans developed as a creole language reflecting the diverse community at the Cape — Dutch settlers, Malay-speaking enslaved people, Portuguese traders, and Khoikhoi workers all contributed.'),
  q(9, 'How did the Dutch settlement at the Cape change from its original purpose?',
    ['It grew from a small refreshment station into a full colony with farms, towns, and enslaved labour, taking land from the Khoikhoi and San', 'It remained a small station and never expanded', 'It was abandoned after ten years', 'The Khoikhoi took control of the settlement'], 0,
    'What began as a small refreshment station in 1652 gradually expanded into a colony. Free burghers started farming, enslaved people were imported, French Huguenots and other settlers arrived, and trekboers pushed deep into the interior. This expansion came at the cost of the Khoikhoi and San, who lost their land and way of life.'),
  t(10, '## Connecting Exploration to Today\n\n### The Legacy of European Exploration in South Africa\n\nThe arrival of European settlers at the Cape in 1652 set in motion a chain of events that shaped South Africa\'s history for centuries:\n\n| Then | Now |\n|------|-----|\n| Khoikhoi and San lost their land | Land restitution and reform remain major issues |\n| Enslaved people were brought to the Cape | Their descendants are part of the diverse Coloured community |\n| Dutch, French, and German settlers arrived | Their descendants are part of the Afrikaner community |\n| Different cultures mixed at the Cape | Cape Town\'s diverse food, music, and language heritage |\n| Europeans saw themselves as superior | The Constitution now guarantees equality for all |\n\n### Why This History Matters\n\n- Understanding how colonisation began helps us understand **why inequality exists today**\n- The dispossession of the Khoikhoi and San is directly connected to the **land question** in modern South Africa\n- The history of slavery at the Cape is part of the story of **human rights** — and why the Bill of Rights is so important\n- Learning about exploration from **multiple perspectives** — European, Khoikhoi, San, enslaved people — helps us build a more **just and honest** understanding of the past\n\nAs South Africans, knowing this history empowers us to work towards the vision of the Constitution: a society based on **equality, human dignity, and freedom** for all.'),
];

