// CHAPTER 4 (History – Term 2): Trading in Human Beings — The Transatlantic
// Slave Trade
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Origins and the Triangular Trade ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Was the Transatlantic Slave Trade?\n\nThe **Transatlantic Slave Trade** was the forced transportation of millions of **African people** across the Atlantic Ocean to work as slaves in the Americas. It lasted from the **early 1500s to the mid-1800s** and is one of the greatest crimes in human history.\n\n### Key Facts\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **Duration** | About 350 years (c. 1500–1850) |\n| **People enslaved** | An estimated **12–15 million** Africans were shipped across the Atlantic |\n| **Deaths** | Millions more died during capture, the march to the coast, and the sea voyage |\n| **Destination** | Mainly the Caribbean, Brazil, and the southern states of North America |\n| **European countries involved** | Portugal, Spain, Britain, France, the Netherlands, and others |\n\n### Why Did It Happen?\n\n- European colonists in the Americas needed **cheap labour** to work on **plantations** (large farms growing sugar, tobacco, cotton, and coffee)\n- They tried enslaving **indigenous Americans**, but many died from European diseases\n- Europeans turned to **Africa** as a source of enslaved labour\n- The slave trade was extremely **profitable** — it made European traders, plantation owners, and entire nations wealthy'),
  q(2, 'Approximately how many African people were forcibly transported across the Atlantic during the slave trade?',
    ['An estimated 12 to 15 million people', 'About 1 million people', 'Less than 100 000 people', 'About 50 million people'], 0,
    'Historians estimate that between 12 and 15 million Africans were shipped across the Atlantic Ocean over about 350 years. Millions more died during capture, the forced march to the coast, and the terrible conditions on slave ships. The true human cost was even higher than the number who survived the voyage.'),
  t(3, '## The Triangular Trade\n\nThe slave trade was part of a larger trading system called the **Triangular Trade** because it formed a triangle across the Atlantic Ocean.\n\n### The Three Legs of the Triangle\n\n| Leg | Route | What Was Carried |\n|-----|-------|------------------|\n| **First leg** | Europe to West Africa | Manufactured goods — guns, cloth, iron, alcohol, beads |\n| **Second leg (Middle Passage)** | West Africa to the Americas | Enslaved African people |\n| **Third leg** | Americas to Europe | Plantation products — sugar, tobacco, cotton, rum, coffee |\n\n### How It Worked\n\n1. European ships sailed to **West Africa** loaded with goods like guns, cloth, and alcohol\n2. These goods were exchanged with African slave traders for **enslaved people** who had been captured in raids or wars\n3. The enslaved people were packed onto ships for the terrible voyage across the Atlantic — this was called the **Middle Passage**\n4. In the Americas, the enslaved people were sold to plantation owners\n5. The ships were then loaded with **sugar, tobacco, cotton**, and other goods produced by slave labour\n6. These goods were shipped back to **Europe**, where they were sold for huge profits\n7. The cycle began again\n\n### Who Profited?\n\n- European **merchants and ship owners** made enormous profits\n- **Plantation owners** in the Americas became very wealthy\n- Some **African rulers and traders** also profited by selling captives\n- European **banks, insurance companies, and cities** (like Liverpool and Bristol) grew rich from the trade'),
  fb(4, 'The slave trade was part of a trading system called the ___ Trade. The voyage of enslaved people from West Africa to the Americas was known as the ___.',
    ['Triangular', 'Middle Passage'],
    'The Triangular Trade connected Europe, Africa, and the Americas in a cycle of trade. The Middle Passage — the second leg from Africa to the Americas — was the most horrific part, with enslaved people crammed into ships for weeks under terrible conditions.'),
  t(5, '## Conditions on Slave Ships — The Middle Passage\n\nThe **Middle Passage** was the journey across the Atlantic Ocean from West Africa to the Americas. It was a nightmare of suffering.\n\n### Conditions on Board\n\n- Enslaved people were **chained together** and packed into the ship\'s hold (below deck)\n- There was barely **60 cm of space** per person — they could not sit up or turn over\n- The voyage lasted **6 to 12 weeks** depending on the route and weather\n- There was very little **fresh air, food, or water**\n- Disease spread quickly — **dysentery, smallpox**, and **scurvy** were common\n- The hold was filthy — enslaved people lay in their own waste\n- Those who died were **thrown overboard**\n\n### The Human Cost\n\n| Statistic | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Death rate** | About **15–20%** of enslaved people died during the Middle Passage |\n| **Total deaths** | An estimated **1.5 to 2 million** people died at sea |\n| **Suicide** | Some enslaved people chose to **jump overboard** rather than endure the conditions |\n| **Resistance** | Enslaved people sometimes **rebelled** on ships, though revolts were usually crushed |\n\n### Tight Packing vs Loose Packing\n\n- **Tight packing**: Cramming as many people as possible into the ship — more died, but more survived to be sold\n- **Loose packing**: Giving slightly more space — fewer died, so more could be sold\n- Both methods treated human beings as **cargo** — objects to be bought and sold for profit'),
  q(6, 'What was the death rate during the Middle Passage?',
    ['About 15 to 20 percent of enslaved people died during the voyage', 'Almost nobody died because the ships were well supplied', 'About 90 percent died', 'Only the elderly and sick died'], 0,
    'An estimated 15 to 20 percent of enslaved Africans died during the Middle Passage due to disease, starvation, dehydration, and the brutal conditions. This means for every five people who boarded a slave ship, roughly one did not survive the journey.'),
  t(7, '## The Role of African Rulers and Traders\n\nThe slave trade was not only carried out by Europeans. Some **African rulers and traders** also played a role.\n\n### How Africans Were Captured\n\n- **Warfare**: Some African kingdoms raided neighbouring peoples and sold captives to European traders\n- **Kidnapping**: Individuals were captured while travelling or working\n- **Debt**: People who could not pay debts were sometimes sold into slavery\n- **Punishment**: In some societies, convicted criminals were sold\n\n### African Rulers Who Participated\n\n- Some powerful kingdoms — like **Dahomey** and **Asante** — became wealthy by trading enslaved people\n- They exchanged captives for **European goods**, especially **guns**\n- The guns were then used to capture more people, creating a terrible cycle\n- However, many African leaders **opposed** the slave trade and tried to stop it\n\n### Important Context\n\n- Slavery existed in Africa before the Europeans arrived, but it was **very different** from the transatlantic slave trade\n- African slavery was often **domestic** — enslaved people could marry, own property, and eventually gain freedom\n- The transatlantic slave trade was **chattel slavery** — enslaved people were treated as property with no rights, and slavery was **hereditary** (children of slaves were also slaves)\n- The **scale** of the transatlantic trade — millions of people over centuries — was unlike anything that had existed before'),
  fb(8, 'Some African kingdoms became wealthy by trading enslaved people to Europeans in exchange for goods, especially ___. The transatlantic form of slavery, where people were treated as property, is called ___ slavery.',
    ['guns', 'chattel'],
    'The trade in guns created a destructive cycle — African rulers needed guns to maintain power, and they obtained guns by selling captives to Europeans. Chattel slavery was uniquely cruel because enslaved people were legally classified as property, had no rights whatsoever, and their children were born into slavery.'),
  q(9, 'How was the transatlantic slave trade different from slavery that existed in Africa before the Europeans arrived?',
    ['Transatlantic slavery was chattel slavery — people were property with no rights, slavery was hereditary, and it operated on an unprecedented scale of millions', 'There was no difference between the two types', 'African slavery was worse than transatlantic slavery', 'Transatlantic slavery only lasted a few years'], 0,
    'Traditional African slavery was often domestic — enslaved people could earn their freedom, marry, and own property. The transatlantic slave trade was fundamentally different: it reduced human beings to property (chattel), was hereditary, operated on a massive scale, was racially based, and lasted for centuries.'),
  t(10, '## West Africa Before the Slave Trade\n\nBefore the slave trade devastated the region, West Africa had **thriving civilisations**.\n\n### Great West African Kingdoms\n\n| Kingdom | Period | Known For |\n|---------|--------|----------|\n| **Ghana** | c. 300–1200 | Gold and salt trade |\n| **Mali** | c. 1235–1600 | Mansa Musa, Timbuktu, scholarship |\n| **Songhai** | c. 1430–1591 | Largest West African empire, great trade |\n| **Benin** | c. 1200–1897 | Advanced metalwork (Benin Bronzes), strong government |\n\n### Effects of the Slave Trade on Africa\n\n- **Population loss**: Millions of young, healthy people were taken from the continent\n- **Warfare**: The slave trade encouraged wars and raids to capture people\n- **Disrupted economies**: Trade shifted from goods to human beings\n- **Weakened societies**: Loss of population weakened African kingdoms and made them vulnerable to later European colonisation\n- **Lasting trauma**: The slave trade left deep scars on African societies that are still felt today\n\nUnderstanding that Africa had great civilisations before the slave trade is essential. The slave trade did not happen because Africa was "backward" — it happened because of European greed and the demand for cheap labour on American plantations.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Abolition and Legacy ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Resistance to Slavery\n\nEnslaved people **never accepted** their enslavement. They resisted in many ways, from small daily acts to large-scale rebellions.\n\n### Forms of Resistance\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|------|----------|\n| **Day-to-day resistance** | Working slowly, breaking tools, pretending to be ill, running away |\n| **Preserving culture** | Keeping African languages, music, stories, and religious practices alive |\n| **Rebellion** | Armed uprisings against slave owners |\n| **Escape** | Running away to form free communities (called **maroon communities**) |\n\n### Major Slave Rebellions\n\n| Rebellion | Date | Details |\n|-----------|------|----------|\n| **Haitian Revolution** | 1791–1804 | The only successful large-scale slave revolt — enslaved people in the French colony of Saint-Domingue overthrew their masters and established **Haiti**, the first free black republic |\n| **Nat Turner\'s Rebellion** | 1831 | An uprising in Virginia, USA, led by an enslaved preacher |\n| **Jamaican Rebellions** | 1831–1832 | The Baptist War — a major uprising that helped end slavery in British colonies |\n\n### The Haitian Revolution\n\n- Led by **Toussaint L\'Ouverture**, a formerly enslaved man\n- Enslaved people in Saint-Domingue rose up against French plantation owners\n- They fought off French, Spanish, and British armies\n- In **1804**, Haiti declared independence — the first country founded by formerly enslaved people\n- The Haitian Revolution terrified slave owners across the Americas'),
  q(2, 'Why was the Haitian Revolution historically significant?',
    ['It was the only successful large-scale slave revolt, establishing Haiti as the first free black republic in 1804', 'It happened in South Africa', 'It was led by European abolitionists', 'It failed and nothing changed'], 0,
    'The Haitian Revolution (1791–1804) was a world-changing event. Enslaved people in the French colony of Saint-Domingue overthrew their masters, defeated French, Spanish, and British armies, and established Haiti as the first independent nation founded by formerly enslaved people. It proved that enslaved people could win their freedom through resistance.'),
  t(3, '## The Abolition Movement\n\n**Abolition** means the ending of slavery. The movement to end the slave trade and slavery grew throughout the late 1700s and 1800s.\n\n### Key Abolitionists\n\n| Person | Role |\n|--------|------|\n| **William Wilberforce** | British politician who campaigned in Parliament for decades to ban the slave trade |\n| **Olaudah Equiano** | A formerly enslaved man whose autobiography shocked the British public |\n| **Harriet Tubman** | An escaped slave in the USA who helped others escape through the **Underground Railroad** |\n| **Frederick Douglass** | A formerly enslaved man who became a powerful speaker and writer against slavery |\n| **Toussaint L\'Ouverture** | Led the Haitian Revolution — showed that enslaved people would fight for freedom |\n\n### How Was Slavery Ended?\n\n| Date | Event |\n|------|-------|\n| **1807** | Britain banned the **slave trade** (buying and selling enslaved people) |\n| **1833** | Britain passed the **Emancipation Act**, freeing all enslaved people in British colonies (effective 1834) |\n| **1834** | Slavery ended at the **Cape Colony** (South Africa) |\n| **1848** | France abolished slavery in its colonies |\n| **1865** | The **13th Amendment** ended slavery in the United States after the Civil War |\n| **1888** | Brazil became the **last country** in the Americas to abolish slavery |\n\n### What Convinced People to End Slavery?\n\n- Slave **revolts** (especially Haiti) made slavery dangerous and expensive\n- Abolitionists used **books, pamphlets, speeches**, and **petitions** to change public opinion\n- Some people argued slavery was morally wrong based on **religious** or **humanitarian** beliefs\n- Economic changes meant **factory workers** were becoming more profitable than slave labour'),
  fb(4, 'In 1807, Britain banned the slave ___. Slavery was ended at the Cape Colony in South Africa in ___.',
    ['trade', '1834'],
    'Britain banned the slave trade in 1807, making it illegal for British ships to carry enslaved people. However, people who were already enslaved remained in bondage until the Emancipation Act of 1833 (effective 1834), which freed all enslaved people in British colonies, including the Cape Colony.'),
  t(5, '## The Underground Railroad\n\nThe **Underground Railroad** was not an actual railroad — it was a secret network of routes, safe houses, and brave people who helped enslaved people escape from the southern United States to freedom in the North and Canada.\n\n### How It Worked\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|----------|\n| **Conductor** | A person who guided enslaved people along the route |\n| **Station** | A safe house where escapees could hide, rest, and eat |\n| **Passenger** | An enslaved person escaping to freedom |\n| **Ticket** | A plan or route for escape |\n\n### Harriet Tubman — "Moses of Her People"\n\n- **Harriet Tubman** escaped from slavery in 1849\n- She returned to the South **13 times** to lead about **70 enslaved people** to freedom\n- She never lost a single passenger\n- She risked her life on every journey — slave catchers were always hunting for her\n- During the American Civil War, she served as a **spy, nurse, and scout** for the Union Army\n- She is sometimes called the **"Moses of her people"** because, like Moses in the Bible, she led people out of slavery\n\n### Courage and Sacrifice\n\nHelping enslaved people escape was **extremely dangerous** — anyone caught could be severely punished, imprisoned, or even killed. Both black and white people risked their lives to help others gain freedom.'),
  q(6, 'Why was Harriet Tubman called the "Moses of her people"?',
    ['Because she led enslaved people out of slavery to freedom, just as Moses led the Israelites out of Egypt', 'Because she was a religious leader', 'Because she wrote a famous book about slavery', 'Because she was the president of the United States'], 0,
    'The comparison to Moses is powerful — just as Moses led the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt, Harriet Tubman risked her life repeatedly to lead enslaved African Americans out of slavery in the South to freedom in the North and Canada. She made 13 dangerous trips and never lost a single person.'),
  t(7, '## The Legacy of the Slave Trade\n\nThe transatlantic slave trade ended over 150 years ago, but its effects are still felt today.\n\n### Lasting Effects\n\n| Area | Legacy |\n|------|--------|\n| **Racism** | The slave trade was justified by the false idea that black people were inferior — this racism persists today |\n| **Poverty** | Africa lost millions of young, productive people, weakening economies for centuries |\n| **Inequality** | Former slave-owning nations became wealthy, while Africa remained exploited |\n| **Cultural loss** | Enslaved people lost their languages, names, and family connections |\n| **Diaspora** | Millions of people of African descent now live across the Americas and Caribbean — the **African diaspora** |\n| **Colonisation** | The slave trade weakened African societies, making them vulnerable to European colonisation in the 1800s |\n\n### Connections to South Africa\n\n- Slavery at the **Cape Colony** (1658–1834) was part of the broader story of human trafficking\n- The descendants of Cape slaves are part of today\'s diverse South African society\n- The **racial classification** system of apartheid echoed the racial ideas used to justify slavery\n- South Africa\'s **Constitution and Bill of Rights** explicitly protect human dignity and prohibit slavery and forced labour'),
  fb(8, 'The spread of people of African descent across the Americas and Caribbean is known as the African ___. Slavery at the Cape Colony lasted from 1658 to ___.',
    ['diaspora', '1834'],
    'The African diaspora — the scattered communities of people of African descent across the world — is a direct result of the slave trade. At the Cape, slavery lasted 176 years, from the first enslaved people arriving in 1658 to emancipation in 1834.'),
  q(9, 'How did the slave trade contribute to the later European colonisation of Africa?',
    ['It weakened African societies by removing millions of people and encouraging warfare, making them vulnerable to European conquest', 'It made Africa stronger and better prepared', 'The slave trade had no connection to colonisation', 'Africa was already colonised before the slave trade'], 0,
    'The slave trade removed an estimated 12–15 million people from Africa over 350 years, disrupted economies, and encouraged wars between African groups. These weakened societies were then more easily conquered by European powers during the Scramble for Africa in the late 1800s. The slave trade and colonisation are directly connected.'),
  t(10, '## Remembering and Learning from the Slave Trade\n\n### Memorials and Remembrance\n\n- The United Nations declared **25 March** as the International Day of Remembrance of the Victims of Slavery\n- **Gorée Island** in Senegal has a museum dedicated to the slave trade\n- In South Africa, the **Slave Lodge** in Cape Town (one of the oldest buildings in the country) is now a museum telling the story of slavery at the Cape\n- Monuments, museums, and memorials around the world ensure that this history is not forgotten\n\n### Why We Must Remember\n\n- To honour the **millions of people** who suffered and died\n- To understand the **roots of racism** and inequality\n- To recognise that **human rights** must be protected — the Bill of Rights exists to prevent such abuses from happening again\n- To learn that ordinary people can make a difference — abolitionists, freedom fighters, and everyday heroes helped end slavery\n\n### Connecting Past and Present\n\n**Modern slavery** still exists today. The International Labour Organization estimates that over **40 million people** worldwide are trapped in modern forms of slavery, including forced labour and human trafficking. South Africa\'s laws (guided by the Constitution) criminalise all forms of slavery and trafficking.\n\nThe story of the slave trade teaches us that we must always stand up for **human dignity** — the foundation of our Constitution and our democracy.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 5 (Geography – Term 3): Climate and Weather
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Understanding Climate and Weather ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Climate vs Weather\n\nPeople often confuse **weather** and **climate**, but they are different things.\n\n### Definitions\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|----------|\n| **Weather** | The condition of the atmosphere at a **specific place** and **time** — it can change from hour to hour |\n| **Climate** | The **average weather** of a place over a **long period** (usually 30 years or more) |\n\n### Examples\n\n- **Weather**: "It is raining in Johannesburg today" or "Cape Town is having a cold, windy day"\n- **Climate**: "Johannesburg has a warm, temperate climate with summer rainfall" or "Cape Town has a Mediterranean climate with wet winters"\n\n### Memory Trick\n\n- **Weather** = What you see when you look out the **window** right now\n- **Climate** = What you **expect** based on the time of year and where you live\n\n### Elements of Weather and Climate\n\nBoth weather and climate are described using the same **elements**:\n\n| Element | What It Measures |\n|---------|------------------|\n| **Temperature** | How hot or cold the air is (measured in °C) |\n| **Rainfall (precipitation)** | How much rain, snow, or hail falls (measured in mm) |\n| **Wind** | The speed and direction of air movement |\n| **Humidity** | How much moisture (water vapour) is in the air |\n| **Air pressure** | The weight of the atmosphere pressing down on the Earth\'s surface |\n| **Cloud cover** | How much of the sky is covered by clouds |'),
  q(2, 'What is the main difference between weather and climate?',
    ['Weather is the short-term condition of the atmosphere at a specific time and place, while climate is the average weather over a long period (30+ years)', 'Weather and climate are exactly the same thing', 'Climate changes every day but weather stays the same', 'Weather can only be measured in summer'], 0,
    'Weather refers to the day-to-day conditions — it might be sunny today and rainy tomorrow. Climate is the pattern of weather averaged over at least 30 years. So while Johannesburg might have a cold day in summer (weather), its climate is still classified as warm and temperate.'),
  t(3, '## Factors Affecting Climate\n\nSeveral factors determine why different places have different climates.\n\n### Key Factors\n\n| Factor | Effect on Climate |\n|--------|-------------------|\n| **Latitude** | Places near the equator are hotter; places near the poles are colder |\n| **Altitude** | Higher places are cooler — temperature drops about **6.5°C for every 1 000 m** you go up |\n| **Distance from the sea** | Places near the sea have milder temperatures; places inland have more extreme temperatures |\n| **Ocean currents** | Warm currents heat the air above them; cold currents cool the air |\n| **Wind and pressure belts** | Prevailing winds bring rain or dry conditions |\n| **Mountain barriers** | Mountains can block rain, creating dry areas on the other side (rain shadow) |\n\n### How These Factors Work in South Africa\n\n| Factor | Example in South Africa |\n|--------|------------------------|\n| **Latitude** | South Africa lies between 22°S and 35°S — it has a warm to temperate climate |\n| **Altitude** | Johannesburg (1 753 m) is cooler than Durban (near sea level), even though Joburg is further north |\n| **Ocean currents** | The warm **Agulhas Current** on the east coast makes KwaZulu-Natal warm and humid; the cold **Benguela Current** on the west coast makes the Western Cape coast cooler and drier |\n| **Rain shadow** | The Drakensberg Mountains block moist air from the east, making the interior of the Free State drier |'),
  fb(4, 'Temperature drops about 6.5°C for every 1 000 m increase in ___. The warm ___ Current on South Africa\'s east coast makes KwaZulu-Natal warm and humid.',
    ['altitude', 'Agulhas'],
    'Altitude has a strong effect on temperature — that is why Johannesburg, at 1 753 m above sea level, can be much cooler than Durban at the coast, even though Johannesburg is further north. The Agulhas Current brings warm water from the Indian Ocean along the east coast, heating the air and bringing moisture that causes rainfall.'),
  t(5, '## Climate Zones of South Africa\n\nSouth Africa has a variety of climates due to its position, altitude, and ocean currents.\n\n### Main Climate Zones\n\n| Zone | Where | Characteristics |\n|------|-------|------------------|\n| **Mediterranean** | Western Cape (Cape Town area) | Hot dry summers, cool wet winters |\n| **Subtropical** | KwaZulu-Natal coast (Durban) | Warm all year, summer rainfall, humid |\n| **Semi-arid** | Northern Cape, parts of Free State | Low rainfall, hot summers, cold winters |\n| **Desert** | Kalahari, Namaqualand | Very low rainfall, extreme temperatures |\n| **Temperate interior** | Gauteng, parts of Mpumalanga | Warm summers with thunderstorms, cool dry winters |\n| **Mountain** | Drakensberg, Cape mountains | Cold winters (snow on peaks), cool summers |\n\n### Rainfall Patterns\n\n| Region | Rainfall Season |\n|--------|------------------|\n| **Western Cape** | Winter rainfall (May–August) |\n| **Most of South Africa** | Summer rainfall (October–March) |\n| **Eastern Cape (south coast)** | Rainfall throughout the year |\n| **Northern Cape (west)** | Very little rainfall at any time |\n\nSouth Africa receives an average of only about **464 mm** of rain per year — well below the world average of **860 mm**. This makes South Africa a **water-scarce country**.'),
  q(6, 'Which climate zone is found in the Western Cape around Cape Town?',
    ['Mediterranean climate — with hot dry summers and cool wet winters', 'Desert climate with no rainfall', 'Subtropical climate with summer rainfall', 'Tropical climate with heavy rainfall all year'], 0,
    'Cape Town and the Western Cape have a Mediterranean climate, which is unusual for Africa. This means they get their rain in winter (May–August) and have hot, dry summers. This is the opposite of most of South Africa, which gets rain in summer.'),
  t(7, '## Weather Instruments\n\nTo measure and record the weather accurately, we use special **weather instruments**.\n\n### Common Weather Instruments\n\n| Instrument | What It Measures | Unit |\n|-----------|------------------|------|\n| **Thermometer** | Temperature | Degrees Celsius (°C) |\n| **Rain gauge** | Rainfall (precipitation) | Millimetres (mm) |\n| **Barometer** | Air pressure (atmospheric pressure) | Hectopascals (hPa) or millibars (mb) |\n| **Wind vane** | Wind direction | Compass direction (N, S, E, W) |\n| **Anemometer** | Wind speed | Kilometres per hour (km/h) |\n| **Hygrometer** | Humidity (moisture in the air) | Percentage (%) |\n\n### Weather Stations\n\n- A **weather station** is a place where weather instruments are kept and readings are taken regularly\n- The **South African Weather Service (SAWS)** operates weather stations across the country\n- Weather data is collected at the same times each day so readings can be compared\n- Modern weather stations also use **satellite images, radar**, and **weather balloons** to track weather patterns\n\n### Taking Accurate Readings\n\n- A thermometer should be placed in a **Stevenson screen** — a white wooden box with slatted sides that lets air flow through but keeps out direct sunlight and rain\n- A rain gauge must be placed on a **flat, open area** away from buildings and trees\n- Readings should be taken at the **same times** every day'),
  fb(8, 'A ___ measures air pressure in hectopascals. Thermometers at weather stations are placed in a ___ screen to protect them from direct sunlight and rain.',
    ['barometer', 'Stevenson'],
    'A barometer measures atmospheric pressure, which is important for predicting weather changes. Rising pressure usually means fair weather, while falling pressure often signals rain or storms. The Stevenson screen is a white, louvred box that allows air to circulate while shielding instruments from direct sunlight and rain.'),
  q(9, 'What does an anemometer measure?',
    ['Wind speed', 'Temperature', 'Rainfall', 'Humidity'], 0,
    'An anemometer measures wind speed, usually in kilometres per hour (km/h). It typically has spinning cups that rotate faster as the wind blows harder. The speed of rotation is converted into a wind speed reading.'),
  t(10, '## Reading a Weather Map\n\nWeather maps (also called **synoptic charts**) show weather conditions over a large area.\n\n### Key Features of a Weather Map\n\n| Feature | What It Shows |\n|---------|---------------|\n| **Isobars** | Lines joining places with the same air pressure — closely spaced isobars mean strong winds |\n| **High pressure (H)** | Associated with clear skies, calm weather, and light winds |\n| **Low pressure (L)** | Associated with clouds, rain, and stronger winds |\n| **Cold front** | A line with blue triangles — cold air pushes under warm air, causing rain and cooler temperatures |\n| **Warm front** | A line with red semicircles — warm air rises over cold air, bringing light rain and warmer temperatures |\n| **Wind direction** | Wind blows from high pressure to low pressure |\n\n### Weather Map Tips for South Africa\n\n- **Cold fronts** typically move from west to east across the country\n- In winter, cold fronts bring rain to the **Western Cape** (winter rainfall region)\n- **High pressure systems** (ridges of high pressure) often sit over the interior, bringing dry, clear weather\n- The **South Indian Ocean High** and **South Atlantic High** are semi-permanent high-pressure systems that influence South Africa\'s weather\n\nBeing able to read a weather map helps you understand **why** the weather is the way it is and what might happen next. The South African Weather Service publishes weather maps daily.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Weather and Climate in Everyday Life ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## How Climate Affects People\'s Lives\n\nClimate has a major effect on how people live — what they eat, what they wear, what kind of houses they build, and how they earn a living.\n\n### Climate and Daily Life in South Africa\n\n| Aspect | How Climate Affects It |\n|--------|------------------------|\n| **Farming** | Climate determines what crops can grow — sugarcane in KZN, wheat in the Western Cape, maize in the Free State |\n| **Water supply** | Low rainfall means South Africa is a water-scarce country |\n| **Housing** | In hot areas, houses have thin walls and large windows; in cold areas, thicker walls and smaller windows |\n| **Clothing** | People in Durban wear light clothing; people in the Drakensberg need warm clothes in winter |\n| **Tourism** | Sunny weather attracts tourists to beaches and game reserves |\n| **Health** | Malaria is found in warm, wet areas (Limpopo, Mpumalanga); heatstroke is a risk in very hot areas |\n\n### Agriculture and Climate\n\nSouth African agriculture depends heavily on climate:\n\n| Crop/Product | Climate Needed | Region |\n|-------------|----------------|--------|\n| **Maize** | Summer rainfall, warm temperatures | Free State, Mpumalanga, North West |\n| **Wheat** | Winter rainfall, cooler temperatures | Western Cape |\n| **Sugarcane** | Subtropical, high rainfall | KwaZulu-Natal |\n| **Grapes/Wine** | Mediterranean climate | Western Cape |\n| **Citrus fruit** | Warm, with irrigation | Limpopo, Eastern Cape |\n| **Livestock** | Semi-arid grasslands | Karoo, Free State |'),
  q(2, 'Why is maize grown mainly in the Free State, Mpumalanga, and North West?',
    ['Because these provinces have summer rainfall and warm temperatures, which maize needs to grow', 'Because maize can only grow at the coast', 'Because these provinces have winter rainfall', 'Because maize does not need any water'], 0,
    'Maize is a summer crop that needs warm temperatures and rain during the growing season (October–March). The Free State, Mpumalanga, and North West get summer rainfall and have suitable soils, making them the maize belt of South Africa. Maize (mielie) is South Africa\'s most important staple food.'),
  t(3, '## Extreme Weather Events\n\nSouth Africa experiences various **extreme weather events** that can be dangerous and destructive.\n\n### Types of Extreme Weather in South Africa\n\n| Event | Description | Where in SA |\n|-------|-------------|-------------|\n| **Droughts** | Extended periods of below-average rainfall | Especially the Northern Cape, Eastern Cape, and parts of KZN |\n| **Floods** | Heavy rainfall causing rivers to overflow | KZN coast, Eastern Cape, Gauteng |\n| **Thunderstorms** | Intense storms with lightning, heavy rain, and sometimes hail | Gauteng and Highveld (most lightning-prone area in the world) |\n| **Heatwaves** | Extended periods of extremely high temperatures | Northern Cape, Limpopo |\n| **Berg winds** | Hot, dry winds blowing from the interior to the coast | Western Cape, KZN |\n| **Snow** | Rare in SA but occurs on mountains and at high altitude | Drakensberg, Sneeuberg, parts of the Free State |\n\n### The Day Zero Water Crisis\n\nIn **2017–2018**, Cape Town experienced a severe drought that nearly made it the first major city in the world to run out of water.\n\n- **Day Zero** was the date when taps would be turned off\n- Residents were limited to **50 litres** of water per person per day\n- The crisis was caused by **three consecutive years of below-average rainfall** combined with a growing population\n- Day Zero was avoided thanks to water saving by residents, but the crisis showed how vulnerable cities are to climate change'),
  fb(4, 'In 2017-2018, Cape Town experienced a severe ___ that nearly made it the first major city to run out of water. Gauteng and the Highveld are among the most ___-prone areas in the world.',
    ['drought', 'lightning'],
    'The Cape Town drought was a wake-up call for South Africa. It showed that even a modern city can run out of water if rainfall drops significantly. The Highveld (including Gauteng) has extremely high lightning activity due to its altitude and the intense summer thunderstorms that build up on hot afternoons.'),
  t(5, '## Climate Change\n\n**Climate change** refers to long-term changes in the Earth\'s climate, including rising temperatures, changing rainfall patterns, and more frequent extreme weather events.\n\n### What Causes Climate Change?\n\n- The burning of **fossil fuels** (coal, oil, gas) releases **greenhouse gases** (especially carbon dioxide — CO₂) into the atmosphere\n- These gases trap heat from the sun, causing the Earth to warm up — this is called the **greenhouse effect**\n- **Deforestation** (cutting down trees) releases stored carbon and reduces the number of trees that absorb CO₂\n- **Agriculture** and **industry** also release greenhouse gases\n\n### Effects of Climate Change in South Africa\n\n| Effect | Impact |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Rising temperatures** | Hotter days, especially in the interior; more heatwaves |\n| **Changing rainfall** | Some areas getting drier (Western Cape), some getting wetter (eastern parts) |\n| **More droughts** | Greater water scarcity in an already water-scarce country |\n| **More intense storms** | Heavier rainfall events causing flooding (e.g., KZN floods, April 2022) |\n| **Rising sea levels** | Coastal cities like Durban and Cape Town could face flooding |\n| **Impact on farming** | Changed growing seasons, crop failures, livestock stress |'),
  q(6, 'What is the greenhouse effect?',
    ['The trapping of heat in the atmosphere by greenhouse gases like carbon dioxide, causing the Earth to warm up', 'The growing of plants in a greenhouse', 'The cooling of the Earth by ice caps', 'A type of weather instrument'], 0,
    'The greenhouse effect is a natural process where certain gases in the atmosphere (like CO₂, methane, and water vapour) trap heat from the sun, keeping the Earth warm enough for life. The problem is that human activities (burning fossil fuels, deforestation) are adding extra greenhouse gases, trapping too much heat and causing global warming.'),
  t(7, '## What South Africa Is Doing About Climate Change\n\n### South Africa\'s Carbon Footprint\n\n- South Africa is the **largest carbon emitter in Africa** and among the top 15 in the world\n- This is mainly because **coal** provides about 80% of South Africa\'s electricity (through Eskom\'s power stations)\n- The country also has large mining and industrial sectors that produce emissions\n\n### Steps Being Taken\n\n| Action | Detail |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Renewable energy** | Building wind farms and solar power plants (especially in the Northern Cape) |\n| **Carbon tax** | A tax on businesses that produce large amounts of greenhouse gases |\n| **Energy efficiency** | Encouraging people and businesses to use less electricity |\n| **Reforestation** | Planting trees to absorb CO₂ |\n| **Water management** | Saving water and developing new water sources |\n| **International agreements** | South Africa is part of the **Paris Agreement** on climate change |\n\n### What You Can Do\n\n- **Save electricity** — switch off lights and appliances when not in use\n- **Save water** — take shorter showers, fix leaking taps\n- **Reduce, reuse, recycle** — create less waste\n- **Plant trees** — trees absorb carbon dioxide\n- **Walk or use public transport** — reduce car emissions\n- **Spread awareness** — educate others about climate change'),
  fb(8, 'South Africa is the largest ___ emitter in Africa. About 80% of South Africa\'s electricity comes from burning ___.',
    ['carbon', 'coal'],
    'South Africa\'s heavy reliance on coal-fired power stations makes it a significant contributor to greenhouse gas emissions. Eskom, the state-owned electricity utility, operates many coal power stations. Transitioning to renewable energy sources like solar and wind is essential for reducing South Africa\'s carbon footprint.'),
  q(9, 'Why is South Africa particularly vulnerable to climate change?',
    ['It is already a water-scarce country, and climate change is expected to bring more droughts, more intense storms, and rising temperatures', 'South Africa has too much water', 'Climate change will not affect South Africa', 'South Africa has no farming industry to be affected'], 0,
    'South Africa already receives below-average rainfall and is classified as water-scarce. Climate change is expected to make droughts more frequent and severe, while also increasing the intensity of storms and floods. This threatens water supplies, agriculture, and the livelihoods of millions of South Africans.'),
  t(10, '## Weather Forecasting and Safety\n\n### How Weather Forecasts Are Made\n\nThe **South African Weather Service (SAWS)** uses various tools to forecast weather:\n\n| Tool | How It\'s Used |\n|------|---------------|\n| **Weather stations** | Collect local data on temperature, rainfall, wind, pressure, and humidity |\n| **Satellites** | Capture images of cloud patterns from space |\n| **Radar** | Detects rain and thunderstorms |\n| **Weather balloons** | Measure conditions high in the atmosphere |\n| **Computer models** | Process data to predict future weather patterns |\n\n### Weather Warnings\n\nSAWS issues colour-coded warnings for dangerous weather:\n\n| Level | Colour | Meaning |\n|-------|--------|----------|\n| **Advisory** | Yellow | Be aware — possible dangerous weather |\n| **Watch** | Orange | Be prepared — dangerous weather is likely |\n| **Warning** | Red | Take action — dangerous weather is expected or occurring |\n\n### Safety Tips for Extreme Weather\n\n- **Thunderstorms**: Stay indoors, avoid open areas and tall trees, do not use electrical appliances\n- **Floods**: Move to higher ground, do not try to cross flooded rivers\n- **Heatwaves**: Stay hydrated, stay in the shade, check on elderly neighbours\n- **Cold snaps**: Keep warm, check for hypothermia symptoms in vulnerable people\n\nUnderstanding weather and climate is not just school knowledge — it is practical knowledge that can **save lives**.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 6 (Geography – Term 3): Natural Resources and Conservation
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Renewable and Non-Renewable Resources ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Are Natural Resources?\n\n**Natural resources** are materials found in nature that people use to survive and improve their lives.\n\n### Types of Natural Resources\n\n| Type | Definition | Examples |\n|------|-----------|----------|\n| **Renewable resources** | Resources that can be replaced naturally or grown again | Water, sunlight, wind, forests, fish |\n| **Non-renewable resources** | Resources that take millions of years to form and will eventually run out | Coal, oil, natural gas, gold, diamonds, iron ore |\n\n### Why Natural Resources Matter\n\nNatural resources provide:\n- **Energy** — coal, oil, and gas for electricity and transport; sunlight and wind for renewable energy\n- **Food** — soil and water for farming; fish from rivers and the ocean\n- **Materials** — minerals for manufacturing; wood for building and paper\n- **Water** — for drinking, farming, industry, and sanitation\n\n### South Africa\'s Natural Resources\n\n| Resource | Importance |\n|----------|------------|\n| **Gold** | SA was once the world\'s largest gold producer; gold mining built Johannesburg |\n| **Platinum** | SA has the world\'s largest platinum reserves (Bushveld Complex) |\n| **Coal** | Provides about 80% of SA\'s electricity; large deposits in Mpumalanga |\n| **Diamonds** | Discovered in Kimberley in 1866; major export product |\n| **Iron ore** | Mined in the Northern Cape; used to make steel |\n| **Manganese** | SA has about 80% of the world\'s manganese reserves |\n| **Water** | A scarce resource — SA\'s rivers, dams, and groundwater |'),
  q(2, 'What is the difference between renewable and non-renewable resources?',
    ['Renewable resources can be replaced naturally, while non-renewable resources take millions of years to form and will eventually run out', 'Renewable resources are more expensive than non-renewable resources', 'Non-renewable resources are better for the environment', 'There is no difference between them'], 0,
    'Renewable resources like water, sunlight, and forests can be naturally replenished within a human lifetime. Non-renewable resources like coal, oil, and minerals were formed over millions of years and cannot be replaced once they are used up. This is why conservation and finding alternatives are so important.'),
  t(3, '## The Impact of Mining in South Africa\n\nMining has played a huge role in South Africa\'s economy and history, but it also causes significant environmental damage.\n\n### Positive Effects of Mining\n\n| Benefit | Explanation |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Jobs** | Mining employs hundreds of thousands of South Africans |\n| **Revenue** | Mining generates income for the country through exports and taxes |\n| **Infrastructure** | Mining led to the building of roads, railways, and towns |\n| **Development** | Mining revenue helps fund schools, hospitals, and public services |\n\n### Negative Effects of Mining\n\n| Problem | Explanation |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Water pollution** | Mine waste contaminates rivers and groundwater (acid mine drainage) |\n| **Air pollution** | Dust and emissions from mining affect air quality |\n| **Land degradation** | Open-pit mines destroy the landscape; mine dumps take up land |\n| **Health risks** | Miners face lung diseases (silicosis, tuberculosis) |\n| **Social problems** | Mining towns often have housing shortages, crime, and inequality |\n| **Non-renewable** | Minerals will eventually run out, leaving communities without income |\n\n### Acid Mine Drainage (AMD)\n\n**Acid mine drainage** is one of the most serious environmental problems in South Africa:\n- When mines are abandoned, they fill with water\n- The water reacts with minerals in the rock to form **acidic water**\n- This toxic water seeps into rivers and groundwater, poisoning water supplies\n- AMD is a major problem in **Gauteng** and **Mpumalanga**'),
  fb(4, 'When abandoned mines fill with water that reacts with minerals to form toxic acidic water, this is called acid mine ___. South Africa has about 80% of the world\'s ___ reserves.',
    ['drainage', 'manganese'],
    'Acid mine drainage (AMD) is a serious environmental problem, especially in Gauteng where old gold mines are leaking acidic water into the water supply. South Africa\'s manganese reserves in the Northern Cape are the largest in the world — manganese is used to make steel stronger.'),
  t(5, '## Water Resources in South Africa\n\nWater is South Africa\'s **most precious resource** — the country is classified as **water-scarce**.\n\n### South Africa\'s Water Situation\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **Average rainfall** | 464 mm per year (world average is 860 mm) |\n| **Rivers** | Many rivers flow only part of the year (seasonal) |\n| **Major rivers** | Orange, Vaal, Limpopo, Tugela, Great Fish |\n| **Dams** | Over 5 000 dams store water (Gariep Dam is the largest) |\n| **Groundwater** | An important source in dry areas |\n| **Water use** | Agriculture uses about 60% of all water; urban areas use about 27% |\n\n### Where Does Our Water Come From?\n\n| Source | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Rivers** | Surface water from rivers (the main source) |\n| **Dams** | Store water for use during dry periods |\n| **Groundwater** | Water underground in rock layers (aquifers) — accessed by boreholes |\n| **Rainfall** | Collected from rooftops in tanks (rainwater harvesting) |\n| **Lesotho Highlands Water Project** | Transfers water from Lesotho to Gauteng — one of the largest engineering projects in Africa |\n\n### Unequal Access to Water\n\n- The Constitution guarantees the right to access **sufficient water** (Section 27)\n- However, millions of South Africans, especially in **rural areas** and **informal settlements**, still lack clean, running water\n- Water inequality is linked to the legacy of **apartheid** — historically disadvantaged communities were neglected'),
  q(6, 'Why is South Africa classified as a water-scarce country?',
    ['It receives only 464 mm of rainfall per year, well below the world average of 860 mm, and many rivers are seasonal', 'It has too many dams', 'It rains every day but the water evaporates', 'It has no rivers at all'], 0,
    'South Africa\'s average rainfall of 464 mm per year is barely half the world average. Many rivers are seasonal, evaporation rates are high, and the growing population and economy demand ever more water. This makes water conservation essential for every South African.'),
  t(7, '## Conservation — Protecting Our Resources\n\n**Conservation** means using natural resources wisely so that they are available for future generations. This is also called **sustainability**.\n\n### Principles of Conservation\n\n| Principle | Meaning |\n|-----------|----------|\n| **Reduce** | Use less of a resource (e.g., use less electricity, take shorter showers) |\n| **Reuse** | Use items again instead of throwing them away (e.g., reuse glass jars) |\n| **Recycle** | Process used materials into new products (e.g., recycle paper, plastic, glass, metal) |\n| **Restore** | Repair damaged environments (e.g., plant trees, clean rivers) |\n\n### Conservation Efforts in South Africa\n\n| Effort | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **National parks** | SANParks manages 19 national parks (Kruger, Table Mountain, Addo Elephant, etc.) |\n| **Marine protected areas** | Protect ocean ecosystems and fish stocks |\n| **Working for Water** | A programme that removes alien invasive plants that use too much water |\n| **Renewable energy** | Solar and wind farms reduce dependence on coal |\n| **Water restrictions** | Municipalities impose water limits during droughts |\n| **Environmental laws** | Laws protect the environment (e.g., National Environmental Management Act) |\n\n### Alien Invasive Plants\n\n**Alien invasive plants** are plants brought from other countries that spread aggressively and use large amounts of water. Examples in SA include:\n- **Black wattle** (from Australia)\n- **Port Jackson** (from Australia)\n- **Water hyacinth** (from South America) — clogs rivers and dams'),
  fb(8, 'The three R\'s of conservation are Reduce, Reuse, and ___. The Working for Water programme removes alien ___ plants that use too much water.',
    ['Recycle', 'invasive'],
    'The three R\'s provide a simple framework for conservation. Alien invasive plants are a major problem in South Africa — they consume much more water than indigenous plants and crowd out native species. The Working for Water programme provides jobs while removing these harmful plants.'),
  q(9, 'What is sustainability?',
    ['Using natural resources wisely so that they are available for future generations', 'Using all resources as fast as possible', 'Only using renewable resources', 'Never using any natural resources at all'], 0,
    'Sustainability means meeting our needs today without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their needs. It involves using resources responsibly, minimising waste and pollution, and finding alternatives to non-renewable resources.'),
  t(10, '## Your Role in Conservation\n\n### What You Can Do at Home and School\n\n| Action | Impact |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Turn off lights** when leaving a room | Saves electricity (less coal burned) |\n| **Take shorter showers** (2 minutes) | Saves water |\n| **Fix dripping taps** | A dripping tap wastes up to 30 litres per day |\n| **Recycle** paper, plastic, glass, and cans | Reduces waste in landfills |\n| **Don\'t litter** | Protects rivers, oceans, and wildlife |\n| **Plant indigenous trees** | They use less water and support local wildlife |\n| **Walk or cycle** to school | Reduces air pollution |\n| **Use a refillable water bottle** | Reduces plastic waste |\n\n### Why Conservation Is Everyone\'s Responsibility\n\n- South Africa\'s natural resources are **not unlimited** — they must be managed carefully\n- The Constitution (Section 24) gives everyone the right to an environment that is **not harmful to health**\n- But with this right comes the **responsibility** to look after the environment\n- Small actions by millions of people add up to make a **big difference**\n\n### The Ubuntu of Conservation\n\nThe African philosophy of **ubuntu** — "I am because we are" — applies to conservation too. We share this planet and its resources with other people and with future generations. Protecting the environment is an act of caring for one another.\n\nEvery resource you save today is a resource available for someone tomorrow.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Water Conservation and Sustainability ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## South Africa\'s Water Crisis\n\nSouth Africa faces a growing **water crisis**. Understanding this crisis is essential for every citizen.\n\n### Key Water Facts for South Africa\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **Annual rainfall** | 464 mm (world average: 860 mm) |\n| **Ranking** | South Africa is the **30th driest country** in the world |\n| **Rivers** | Most rivers are seasonal (they dry up in winter or during droughts) |\n| **Population growth** | More people means more demand for water |\n| **Pollution** | Mining, industry, and sewage pollute many rivers and groundwater sources |\n| **Climate change** | Expected to bring more droughts and unreliable rainfall |\n| **Infrastructure** | Ageing pipes lose up to 30-40% of treated water through leaks |\n\n### Major Dams in South Africa\n\n| Dam | Province | River |\n|-----|----------|-------|\n| **Gariep Dam** | Free State/Eastern Cape border | Orange River |\n| **Vanderkloof Dam** | Northern Cape | Orange River |\n| **Sterkfontein Dam** | Free State | Connected to the Tugela-Vaal Transfer Scheme |\n| **Vaal Dam** | Gauteng/Free State | Vaal River |\n| **Theewaterskloof Dam** | Western Cape | Main supply for Cape Town |\n\nThe **Lesotho Highlands Water Project** is a massive scheme that transfers water from Lesotho\'s mountains through tunnels to the **Vaal River system**, supplying Gauteng with much of its water.'),
  q(2, 'Why does the Lesotho Highlands Water Project exist?',
    ['To transfer water from Lesotho to Gauteng, which needs more water than its own rivers can supply', 'To generate electricity for Lesotho only', 'To prevent floods in Lesotho', 'To irrigate farms in the Free State'], 0,
    'Gauteng is the most populous and economically productive province in South Africa, but it does not have enough local water sources to meet demand. The Lesotho Highlands Water Project transfers water from the rainy mountains of Lesotho through tunnels to the Vaal River system, which supplies Gauteng. It is one of the largest water transfer schemes in the world.'),
  t(3, '## Catchment Areas and River Health\n\n### What Is a Catchment Area?\n\nA **catchment area** (also called a watershed or drainage basin) is an area of land where all the rainfall drains into a single river system.\n\n- South Africa has **19 main catchment areas** (called Water Management Areas)\n- Everything that happens in a catchment area affects the river — farming, mining, towns, factories\n- If you pollute the land in a catchment, you pollute the river\n\n### River Health in South Africa\n\nMany of South Africa\'s rivers are in **poor condition**:\n\n| Problem | Cause |\n|---------|-------|\n| **Sewage pollution** | Broken sewage works release untreated sewage into rivers |\n| **Acid mine drainage** | Old mines leak toxic acidic water |\n| **Industrial waste** | Factories dump chemicals into rivers |\n| **Agricultural runoff** | Fertilisers and pesticides wash into rivers |\n| **Alien invasive plants** | Water hyacinth and other plants block waterways |\n| **Litter** | Plastic and rubbish ends up in rivers and eventually the ocean |\n\n### The Orange-Vaal River System\n\nThe **Orange River** is South Africa\'s longest river (2 200 km). Together with the **Vaal River**, it forms the largest river system in South Africa, providing water for:\n- **Gauteng** (South Africa\'s economic heartland)\n- Large parts of the **Free State** and **Northern Cape**\n- Irrigation for agriculture along its banks'),
  fb(4, 'A ___ area is an area of land where all rainfall drains into a single river system. The Orange River is South Africa\'s ___ river at 2 200 km.',
    ['catchment', 'longest'],
    'A catchment area (or watershed) is like a giant funnel — all rain that falls in the area eventually ends up in the same river. This means that pollution anywhere in the catchment affects the river downstream. The Orange River flows across nearly the entire width of South Africa.'),
  t(5, '## Saving Water — Practical Strategies\n\n### Why Every Drop Counts\n\nIn a water-scarce country like South Africa, **every person** must help save water. Small changes in daily habits can make a huge difference when multiplied by millions of people.\n\n### Water-Saving Tips\n\n| Where | How to Save |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Bathroom** | Take 2-minute showers instead of baths; turn off the tap while brushing teeth |\n| **Kitchen** | Use a basin to wash dishes, not a running tap; only run the dishwasher when full |\n| **Garden** | Water early morning or late afternoon (less evaporation); use mulch; plant water-wise indigenous plants |\n| **Laundry** | Only run the washing machine with a full load |\n| **Leaks** | Fix dripping taps immediately — one drip per second wastes about 30 litres per day |\n| **Car** | Use a bucket instead of a hose to wash the car |\n| **Toilet** | Place a filled bottle in the cistern to reduce water per flush |\n\n### Rainwater Harvesting\n\n- Install a **rain tank** to collect water from your roof\n- Rainwater can be used for **gardens, washing cars, flushing toilets**, and even drinking (with proper filtering)\n- A single storm can fill a large tank, providing free water for weeks\n\n### Grey Water Recycling\n\n**Grey water** is water from baths, showers, and washing machines (not toilet water). It can be reused for watering gardens, reducing fresh water demand.'),
  q(6, 'How much water can a dripping tap waste per day?',
    ['About 30 litres', 'Less than 1 litre', 'About 500 litres', 'A dripping tap does not waste any water'], 0,
    'A tap dripping at one drop per second wastes about 30 litres of water every day — that is over 10 000 litres per year from a single drip! Fixing leaks is one of the easiest and most effective ways to save water.'),
  t(7, '## Wetlands — Nature\'s Water Filters\n\n**Wetlands** are areas where the land is covered by water for all or part of the year. They are among the most important ecosystems on Earth.\n\n### Types of Wetlands in South Africa\n\n| Type | Description | Example |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| **Marshes** | Grassy areas with standing water | Nylsvley (Limpopo) |\n| **Swamps** | Wetlands with trees and shrubs | Kosi Bay swamp forests (KZN) |\n| **Vleis/Pans** | Shallow seasonal lakes | Pans of the Free State |\n| **Estuaries** | Where rivers meet the sea | St Lucia Estuary (KZN) |\n| **Floodplains** | Flat areas along rivers that flood seasonally | Okavango Delta (Botswana) |\n\n### Why Wetlands Matter\n\n| Function | How It Helps |\n|----------|---------------|\n| **Water filtration** | Wetlands filter pollutants and sediment from water — like natural kidneys |\n| **Flood control** | They absorb excess water during heavy rains, reducing flooding |\n| **Groundwater recharge** | Water seeps from wetlands into underground aquifers |\n| **Biodiversity** | Wetlands support a huge variety of plants, birds, fish, and other animals |\n| **Food and resources** | Reeds, fish, and plants from wetlands support local communities |\n\n### Threats to Wetlands\n\n- **Drainage** for farming or building\n- **Pollution** from mines, farms, and towns\n- **Overgrazing** by livestock\n- **Invasive plants** that choke wetlands\n- Over **50% of South Africa\'s wetlands** have been lost or degraded'),
  fb(8, 'Wetlands act as natural ___, filtering pollutants and sediment from water. Over 50% of South Africa\'s wetlands have been ___ or degraded.',
    ['kidneys', 'lost'],
    'Wetlands are often called "nature\'s kidneys" because they filter water naturally. The loss of over half of South Africa\'s wetlands is a serious environmental problem because it reduces the country\'s ability to naturally purify water, control floods, and support biodiversity.'),
  q(9, 'Why are wetlands sometimes called "nature\'s kidneys"?',
    ['Because they filter pollutants and sediment from water, just as kidneys filter waste from blood', 'Because they are shaped like kidneys', 'Because they are found near hospitals', 'Because animals drink water there'], 0,
    'Just as kidneys filter waste products from your blood to keep you healthy, wetlands filter pollutants, sediment, and excess nutrients from water as it passes through. This natural filtration process helps keep rivers and groundwater clean, which is essential for drinking water and ecosystem health.'),
  t(10, '## The Future of Water in South Africa\n\n### Growing Challenges\n\n| Challenge | Why It\'s Getting Worse |\n|-----------|------------------------|\n| **Population growth** | More people need more water |\n| **Urbanisation** | Growing cities put pressure on water infrastructure |\n| **Climate change** | Less reliable rainfall, more droughts |\n| **Pollution** | Mining, industrial, and sewage pollution of water sources |\n| **Ageing infrastructure** | Old pipes and water treatment works need billions of rands in repairs |\n\n### Solutions for the Future\n\n| Solution | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Desalination** | Removing salt from seawater to produce fresh water — expensive but becoming cheaper |\n| **Water recycling** | Treating used water so it can be used again (direct water reuse) |\n| **Fixing leaks** | Some municipalities lose 30-40% of treated water through pipe leaks |\n| **Groundwater** | Exploring and sustainably using underground water sources |\n| **Better farming** | Drip irrigation and other water-efficient farming methods |\n| **Education** | Teaching people to value and save water |\n\n### Water Is Life\n\nIn many South African languages, there are sayings that water is life:\n- *Amanzi yimpilo* (isiZulu) — Water is life\n- *Meetse ke bophelo* (Sesotho) — Water is life\n\nIn a country where many communities still walk kilometres to collect water, understanding water conservation is not just academic — it is a matter of **survival and justice**. The Constitution guarantees the right to water, and it is everyone\'s responsibility to protect this precious resource.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 7 (Geography – Term 4): Population
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: Population Concepts and Distribution ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Population?\n\n**Population** refers to the total number of people living in a particular area at a particular time.\n\n### Key Population Terms\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Population size** | The total number of people in a country or area |\n| **Population growth** | The increase (or decrease) in the number of people over time |\n| **Birth rate** | The number of babies born per 1 000 people per year |\n| **Death rate** | The number of deaths per 1 000 people per year |\n| **Natural increase** | Birth rate minus death rate — tells us how fast the population is growing naturally |\n| **Population density** | The number of people per square kilometre (km²) |\n| **Population distribution** | How people are spread out across an area |\n\n### Calculating Natural Increase\n\n**Natural increase = Birth rate − Death rate**\n\n*Example:*\n- Birth rate = 22 per 1 000\n- Death rate = 9 per 1 000\n- Natural increase = 22 − 9 = **13 per 1 000** (or 1.3%)\n\nIf the birth rate is higher than the death rate, the population **grows**.\nIf the death rate is higher than the birth rate, the population **declines**.'),
  q(2, 'If a country has a birth rate of 25 per 1 000 and a death rate of 10 per 1 000, what is the natural increase?',
    ['15 per 1 000 (or 1.5%)', '35 per 1 000', '250 per 1 000', '2.5 per 1 000'], 0,
    'Natural increase is calculated by subtracting the death rate from the birth rate: 25 − 10 = 15 per 1 000. This means for every 1 000 people, the population grows by 15 each year (a growth rate of 1.5%). This tells us how fast the population is growing without considering migration.'),
  t(3, '## Population Distribution in South Africa\n\n**Population distribution** describes how people are spread out across an area. In South Africa, the population is very **unevenly distributed**.\n\n### South Africa\'s Population (2022 Census)\n\n| Province | Population (million) | Share of National |\n|----------|---------------------|--------------------|\n| **Gauteng** | 16.1 | 26% |\n| **KwaZulu-Natal** | 11.5 | 19% |\n| **Western Cape** | 7.4 | 12% |\n| **Eastern Cape** | 6.7 | 11% |\n| **Limpopo** | 5.8 | 9% |\n| **Mpumalanga** | 4.7 | 8% |\n| **North West** | 4.1 | 7% |\n| **Free State** | 2.9 | 5% |\n| **Northern Cape** | 1.3 | 2% |\n| **Total** | ~60.6 million | 100% |\n\n### Why Is the Distribution Uneven?\n\n| Factor | Effect |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Jobs** | Gauteng attracts people because it has the most jobs (mining, industry, services) |\n| **Water** | Areas with more water can support more people |\n| **Climate** | Comfortable climates attract more people |\n| **Fertile soil** | Good farming areas support denser populations |\n| **History** | Apartheid forced black South Africans into **homelands** and townships |\n| **Infrastructure** | Cities have better roads, schools, hospitals, and services |'),
  fb(4, '___ is South Africa\'s most populous province with about 16.1 million people. The ___ Cape is the least populous province.',
    ['Gauteng', 'Northern'],
    'Gauteng is by far South Africa\'s most densely populated province. Despite being the smallest province by area, it has the most people because Johannesburg and Pretoria/Tshwane provide the most economic opportunities. The Northern Cape is the largest province by area but has the fewest people because it is mostly semi-arid and desert.'),
  t(5, '## Rural vs Urban Areas\n\n### Definitions\n\n| Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Rural areas** | Countryside areas with low population density — farms, villages, small towns |\n| **Urban areas** | Cities and large towns with high population density |\n\n### Urbanisation in South Africa\n\n**Urbanisation** is the movement of people from rural areas to cities. South Africa has experienced rapid urbanisation:\n\n| Year | Urban Population |\n|------|------------------|\n| 1950 | 42% |\n| 1990 | 52% |\n| 2020 | 68% |\n| 2030 (projected) | 75% |\n\n### Rural vs Urban — Advantages and Disadvantages\n\n| Aspect | Rural Areas | Urban Areas |\n|--------|-------------|-------------|\n| **Jobs** | Limited — mainly farming | Many different jobs available |\n| **Services** | Fewer schools, hospitals, shops | Better schools, hospitals, shops |\n| **Cost of living** | Lower | Higher (rent, food, transport) |\n| **Space** | More space, less crowded | Crowded, especially in informal settlements |\n| **Environment** | Cleaner air, natural surroundings | Pollution, noise, traffic |\n| **Safety** | Generally safer | Higher crime rates |\n| **Opportunities** | Limited education and career options | More education and career options |'),
  q(6, 'What does urbanisation mean?',
    ['The movement of people from rural areas to cities and towns', 'The building of new farms in the countryside', 'People moving from cities to rural areas', 'The growth of forests near towns'], 0,
    'Urbanisation is the process of people moving from rural areas (the countryside) to urban areas (cities and towns). In South Africa, about 68% of people now live in urban areas, mainly because cities offer more job opportunities, better education, and improved services.'),
  t(7, '## Migration — Push and Pull Factors\n\n**Migration** is the movement of people from one place to another. People migrate for many reasons, which can be divided into **push factors** (reasons to leave) and **pull factors** (reasons to go).\n\n### Push and Pull Factors\n\n| Push Factors (Why People Leave) | Pull Factors (Why People Go There) |\n|--------------------------------|------------------------------------|\n| Unemployment / no jobs | Job opportunities |\n| Poverty | Higher wages |\n| Poor services (schools, hospitals) | Better services |\n| Drought or natural disasters | Better climate |\n| War or conflict | Safety and peace |\n| Discrimination | Freedom and equality |\n| Crime and violence | Safety |\n\n### Types of Migration in South Africa\n\n| Type | Example |\n|------|----------|\n| **Rural to urban** | People moving from the Eastern Cape to Johannesburg for work |\n| **Urban to urban** | People moving from a small town to Cape Town |\n| **International** | People from Zimbabwe, Mozambique, or Nigeria coming to South Africa |\n| **Seasonal** | Farm workers who move to areas where crops need harvesting |\n\n### Effects of Migration\n\n- **On sending areas**: Loss of young workers, families left behind, but money (remittances) sent home\n- **On receiving areas**: Population growth, pressure on housing and services, cultural diversity, but also economic contribution and innovation'),
  fb(8, 'Reasons that make people leave an area are called ___ factors. Reasons that attract people to a new area are called ___ factors.',
    ['push', 'pull'],
    'Push factors drive people away from their current location (e.g., unemployment, poverty, drought), while pull factors attract them to a new place (e.g., jobs, better services, safety). Migration usually involves both push and pull factors working together.'),
  q(9, 'Which type of migration is most common in South Africa?',
    ['Rural to urban migration — people moving from the countryside to cities for jobs and better services', 'Urban to rural migration', 'Everyone moving to the Northern Cape', 'International migration to Antarctica'], 0,
    'Rural to urban migration is the most common form of migration in South Africa. People move from rural areas (especially the Eastern Cape, Limpopo, and KwaZulu-Natal) to cities like Johannesburg, Cape Town, and Durban because cities offer more jobs, higher wages, and better access to education, healthcare, and other services.'),
  t(10, '## Population and the Census\n\n### What Is a Census?\n\nA **census** is an official count of all the people in a country. In South Africa, the census is conducted by **Statistics South Africa (Stats SA)**.\n\n### South African Census History\n\n| Year | Key Findings |\n|------|---------------|\n| **1996** | First post-apartheid census — 40.6 million people |\n| **2001** | 44.8 million people |\n| **2011** | 51.8 million people |\n| **2022** | 60.6 million people |\n\n### What Does a Census Measure?\n\n- **Population size** — how many people live in South Africa\n- **Age** — how old people are (used to plan schools, pensions)\n- **Gender** — male and female distribution\n- **Location** — where people live (urban vs rural, provinces)\n- **Languages** — which languages people speak at home\n- **Education** — how many people have completed school or university\n- **Employment** — how many people have jobs\n- **Housing** — what kind of homes people live in, access to water and electricity\n\n### Why Is a Census Important?\n\nThe government uses census data to:\n- **Plan services** — build schools, hospitals, and roads where they are needed\n- **Allocate budgets** — spend money where the most people live\n- **Track progress** — measure whether life is improving for all South Africans\n- **Make policies** — design programmes to address poverty, unemployment, and inequality\n\nCensus data helps ensure that the government serves **all** its people — not just those in wealthy urban areas.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Migration and Urbanisation Challenges ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## Urbanisation Challenges in South Africa\n\nAs more people move to cities, South Africa faces growing **urbanisation challenges**.\n\n### Informal Settlements\n\n**Informal settlements** (also called shack settlements or squatter camps) are areas where people build homes from corrugated iron, wood, and other materials without formal planning.\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **Number** | Over 2 700 informal settlements across South Africa |\n| **People** | Millions of South Africans live in informal settlements |\n| **Location** | Found on the edges of major cities — Johannesburg, Cape Town, Durban, Pretoria |\n| **Names** | Khayelitsha (Cape Town), Diepsloot (Johannesburg), Umlazi (Durban) |\n\n### Conditions in Informal Settlements\n\n| Challenge | Explanation |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Housing** | Homes made of scrap materials — not waterproof, fire risk |\n| **Water** | Limited access to clean water — often shared communal taps |\n| **Sanitation** | Few or no proper toilets — shared facilities or open defecation |\n| **Electricity** | Many homes have illegal connections, risking electrocution and fires |\n| **Flooding** | Settlements are often on low-lying land that floods during heavy rain |\n| **Fire** | Shack fires are common and spread quickly in densely packed areas |\n| **Crime** | Overcrowding and poverty can lead to high crime rates |\n| **Health** | Diseases spread easily due to overcrowding and poor sanitation |'),
  q(2, 'Why do informal settlements exist in South Africa?',
    ['Because many people moving to cities cannot afford formal housing, and government housing delivery has not kept up with demand', 'Because people prefer living in shacks', 'Because the government encourages informal settlements', 'Because there is too much housing in cities'], 0,
    'Informal settlements are a result of rapid urbanisation combined with a shortage of affordable housing. People migrate to cities for jobs and opportunities but cannot afford formal housing. The government\'s housing programme has delivered millions of houses since 1994, but demand still far outstrips supply.'),
  t(3, '## Services and Infrastructure in Cities\n\n### Service Delivery\n\nSouth Africa\'s cities struggle to provide basic services to all residents.\n\n| Service | Challenge |\n|---------|----------|\n| **Water** | Many informal settlements lack piped water |\n| **Electricity** | Load shedding affects everyone; informal areas lack formal connections |\n| **Sanitation** | Millions lack proper toilets |\n| **Roads** | Traffic congestion in cities; informal areas have unpaved roads |\n| **Transport** | Public transport is limited; many people spend hours commuting |\n| **Waste removal** | Informal settlements often lack regular refuse collection |\n\n### Service Delivery Protests\n\n- Many communities protest when services are not delivered — these are called **service delivery protests**\n- Protests have increased in recent years as people demand water, electricity, housing, and roads\n- The Constitution guarantees rights to housing (Section 26), water (Section 27), and a clean environment (Section 24)\n- However, providing these services to a growing urban population is an enormous challenge\n\n### The Housing Challenge\n\n| Fact | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **RDP/BNG housing** | The government has built over 4 million free houses since 1994 |\n| **Housing backlog** | About 2.3 million households are still on waiting lists |\n| **Land** | Finding suitable land near cities for housing is difficult and expensive |\n| **Quality** | Some government-built houses have been criticised for poor quality |'),
  fb(4, 'The government has built over ___ million free houses since 1994. However, about 2.3 million households are still on ___ lists.',
    ['4', 'waiting'],
    'Since the end of apartheid, the government has delivered over 4 million free houses (originally called RDP houses) to low-income families. Despite this massive effort, the housing backlog remains at about 2.3 million due to population growth, continued migration to cities, and the slow pace of construction.'),
  t(5, '## Population Growth and Its Implications\n\n### South Africa\'s Population Growth\n\n| Year | Population | Annual Growth Rate |\n|------|-----------|--------------------|\n| 1994 | 40 million | 2.1% |\n| 2000 | 45 million | 1.5% |\n| 2010 | 50 million | 1.2% |\n| 2022 | 60.6 million | 1.2% |\n\n### Factors Affecting Population Growth in South Africa\n\n| Factor | Effect |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Better healthcare** | More people survive childhood; people live longer |\n| **HIV/AIDS** | Slowed population growth in the 2000s; treatment has improved survival since |\n| **Education** | Better-educated people tend to have fewer children |\n| **Urbanisation** | People in cities tend to have fewer children than those in rural areas |\n| **Immigration** | People from other African countries add to the population |\n\n### Implications of Population Growth\n\n| Area | Challenge |\n|------|----------|\n| **Jobs** | More people need employment — youth unemployment is already over 60% |\n| **Education** | More schools and teachers are needed |\n| **Healthcare** | More hospitals and clinics are needed |\n| **Housing** | More homes need to be built |\n| **Food** | More food needs to be produced |\n| **Water** | More water is needed in an already water-scarce country |\n| **Environment** | More people put more pressure on natural resources |'),
  q(6, 'Why is youth unemployment a major concern related to population growth in South Africa?',
    ['Because South Africa has a young population and over 60% of young people are unemployed, meaning more young people are entering the job market each year', 'Because there are too many jobs and not enough workers', 'Because young people do not want to work', 'Youth unemployment is not a problem in South Africa'], 0,
    'South Africa has a young population — a large proportion of its people are under 35. With youth unemployment exceeding 60%, this creates enormous social and economic pressure. Each year, hundreds of thousands of young people enter the job market, but the economy is not creating enough jobs for them.'),
  t(7, '## International Migration to South Africa\n\nSouth Africa attracts many migrants from other African countries and beyond.\n\n### Why People Migrate to South Africa\n\n| Push Factors (From Home Country) | Pull Factors (To South Africa) |\n|---------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| War and conflict (DRC, Somalia) | Peace and stability |\n| Poverty (Zimbabwe, Mozambique) | Better job opportunities |\n| Political persecution | Democratic government |\n| Limited opportunities | Better education and healthcare |\n| Famine and drought | More food security |\n\n### Contributions of International Migrants\n\nMigrants make important contributions to South Africa:\n- They fill **skills gaps** in healthcare, engineering, and other professions\n- They create **businesses** and employ South Africans\n- They bring **cultural diversity** — food, music, languages\n- They contribute to the **economy** through taxes and spending\n\n### Xenophobia — A Serious Problem\n\n**Xenophobia** means fear and hatred of foreigners. South Africa has experienced violent xenophobic attacks:\n- **2008** — Widespread attacks on foreign nationals; over 60 people killed\n- **2015** — Attacks in KwaZulu-Natal and Gauteng\n- **2019** — Violence against foreign-owned shops in Johannesburg\n\nXenophobia is driven by competition for jobs, housing, and services. However, attacking foreigners is a **crime** and a violation of **human rights**. The Constitution protects the rights of **all people** in South Africa, not just citizens.'),
  fb(8, '___ means fear and hatred of foreigners. In 2008, widespread xenophobic attacks in South Africa resulted in over ___ people being killed.',
    ['Xenophobia', '60'],
    'Xenophobia is a serious human rights problem in South Africa. The 2008 attacks were the worst, with over 60 people killed and thousands displaced. The Constitution protects the rights of all people in South Africa, including non-citizens, and violence against foreigners is both morally wrong and a criminal offence.'),
  q(9, 'How does the South African Constitution relate to the rights of foreign nationals?',
    ['The Constitution protects the rights of ALL people in South Africa, not just citizens — including the right to dignity, life, and freedom from violence', 'The Constitution only protects South African citizens', 'Foreign nationals have no rights in South Africa', 'The Constitution encourages xenophobia'], 0,
    'The South African Bill of Rights applies to "everyone" — not just citizens. This means foreign nationals in South Africa have the right to human dignity, life, freedom from violence, and other fundamental rights. Certain rights (like voting) are limited to citizens, but basic human rights belong to all people.'),
  t(10, '## Population and Development\n\n### What Is Development?\n\n**Development** means improving people\'s quality of life — not just making money, but ensuring that everyone has access to education, healthcare, housing, clean water, and opportunities.\n\n### Measuring Development\n\n| Indicator | What It Measures | SA\'s Status |\n|-----------|-----------------|-------------|\n| **GDP per capita** | Average income per person | Upper-middle income country |\n| **Human Development Index (HDI)** | Life expectancy, education, income | Medium human development |\n| **Life expectancy** | How long people live on average | About 65 years |\n| **Literacy rate** | Percentage of people who can read and write | About 95% |\n| **Infant mortality** | Deaths of babies under 1 year per 1 000 births | About 23 per 1 000 |\n| **Access to water** | Percentage with clean water access | About 89% |\n| **Gini coefficient** | Measures inequality (0 = perfect equality, 1 = perfect inequality) | 0.63 (one of the highest in the world) |\n\n### South Africa\'s Development Challenge\n\nSouth Africa is a country of **contrasts**:\n- It has a modern economy with world-class infrastructure\n- But it also has extreme poverty, unemployment, and inequality\n- The **Gini coefficient of 0.63** makes South Africa one of the most **unequal countries** in the world\n- Addressing this inequality — through education, job creation, and improved services — is the country\'s greatest challenge\n\nPopulation studies help us understand these challenges and plan solutions. Every person counted in a census represents real needs — for water, food, education, healthcare, and opportunity.'),
];

// =============================================================================
// CHAPTER 8 (Geography – Term 4): Trade and Development
// =============================================================================

// --- Lesson 1: What Is Trade? ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, '## Understanding Trade\n\n**Trade** is the buying and selling of goods and services. Trade has existed since ancient times — from the trans-Saharan trade we studied in Chapter 2 to the global trade networks of today.\n\n### Types of Trade\n\n| Type | Definition | Example |\n|------|-----------|----------|\n| **Local trade** | Buying and selling within a town or city | Buying vegetables at a local market |\n| **National trade** | Trade between different parts of the same country | Selling Cape Town wine in Johannesburg |\n| **International trade** | Trade between different countries | SA exporting gold to India |\n\n### Imports and Exports\n\n| Term | Definition | Example |\n|------|-----------|----------|\n| **Imports** | Goods bought from other countries | SA imports oil from Saudi Arabia |\n| **Exports** | Goods sold to other countries | SA exports platinum to China |\n| **Trade balance** | The difference between exports and imports | If exports > imports = trade surplus |\n| **Trade deficit** | When a country imports more than it exports | More money going out than coming in |\n\n### Why Countries Trade\n\n- No country can produce **everything** it needs\n- Countries specialise in producing what they are **best at** (comparative advantage)\n- Trade allows countries to access goods and resources they **don\'t have**\n- Trade creates **jobs** and generates **income**\n- Trade encourages **competition**, which can lead to better quality and lower prices'),
  q(2, 'What is the difference between imports and exports?',
    ['Imports are goods bought from other countries, while exports are goods sold to other countries', 'Imports are goods made locally, exports are goods made abroad', 'There is no difference', 'Imports are more expensive than exports'], 0,
    'Imports are goods and services that a country buys from other countries (they come IN to the country). Exports are goods and services that a country sells to other countries (they go OUT of the country). A country\'s trade balance is the difference between its exports and imports.'),
  t(3, '## South Africa\'s Trade\n\nSouth Africa is the most industrialised country in Africa and a major player in international trade.\n\n### What Does South Africa Export?\n\n| Product | Share of Exports |\n|---------|------------------|\n| **Mining products** | Gold, platinum, iron ore, manganese, coal, diamonds |\n| **Vehicles and parts** | Cars, trucks (BMW, Toyota, Mercedes-Benz have factories in SA) |\n| **Agricultural products** | Fruit (citrus, grapes), wine, sugar, maize |\n| **Processed goods** | Steel, chemicals, machinery |\n\n### What Does South Africa Import?\n\n| Product | Why We Import It |\n|---------|------------------|\n| **Crude oil** | SA has very little oil; we import it for petrol and diesel |\n| **Machinery and equipment** | For factories, mining, and construction |\n| **Electronics** | Phones, computers, televisions |\n| **Vehicles** | Some car brands not manufactured in SA |\n| **Pharmaceuticals** | Medicines not produced locally |\n\n### South Africa\'s Main Trading Partners\n\n| Partner | Relationship |\n|---------|---------------|\n| **China** | SA\'s largest trading partner — buys our minerals, sells us electronics and manufactured goods |\n| **European Union** | Major market for SA fruit, wine, and vehicles |\n| **United States** | Imports SA minerals and exports technology |\n| **India** | Growing trade relationship |\n| **African countries** | SA trades with neighbours through **SADC** (Southern African Development Community) |'),
  fb(4, 'South Africa\'s largest trading partner is ___. South Africa has very little ___ and must import it for petrol and diesel.',
    ['China', 'oil'],
    'China has become South Africa\'s largest trading partner, buying large quantities of iron ore, manganese, platinum, and other minerals. South Africa must import almost all of its crude oil because it has very limited oil reserves, making the country vulnerable to changes in global oil prices.'),
  t(5, '## Trade Organisations and Agreements\n\n### Regional Trade\n\n| Organisation | What It Is |\n|-------------|------------|\n| **SADC** | Southern African Development Community — 16 countries in southern Africa working together on trade, development, and security |\n| **African Union (AU)** | Organisation of all 55 African countries promoting unity and development |\n| **AfCFTA** | African Continental Free Trade Area — a trade agreement covering the entire continent, creating the largest free trade area in the world |\n\n### International Trade\n\n| Organisation | What It Does |\n|-------------|---------------|\n| **World Trade Organization (WTO)** | Sets rules for international trade between countries |\n| **BRICS** | Group of major emerging economies — **B**razil, **R**ussia, **I**ndia, **C**hina, **S**outh Africa — plus new members |\n\n### South Africa in BRICS\n\n- South Africa joined BRICS in **2010**\n- BRICS members represent about **40%** of the world\'s population\n- Membership gives South Africa access to large markets and investment\n- The **BRICS New Development Bank** funds infrastructure projects in member countries\n\n### Trade Barriers\n\n| Barrier | Explanation |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Tariffs** | Taxes on imported goods (make imports more expensive to protect local industries) |\n| **Quotas** | Limits on the amount of a product that can be imported |\n| **Subsidies** | Government payments to local producers that give them an unfair advantage |\n| **Sanctions** | Trade restrictions imposed on a country for political reasons |'),
  q(6, 'What is the AfCFTA?',
    ['The African Continental Free Trade Area — a trade agreement covering the entire African continent, creating the world\'s largest free trade area', 'An American trade company', 'A South African mining company', 'A European trade agreement'], 0,
    'The African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA) is a landmark agreement signed by most African countries. It aims to create a single market for goods and services across Africa, boosting trade between African countries, creating jobs, and reducing the continent\'s dependence on exporting raw materials.'),
  t(7, '## Fair Trade\n\n**Fair trade** is a trading partnership that aims to give producers in developing countries a **fair price** for their goods, better working conditions, and support for their communities.\n\n### How Fair Trade Works\n\n| Principle | Explanation |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Fair prices** | Producers receive a guaranteed minimum price that covers their costs of production |\n| **Fair wages** | Workers are paid decent wages and work reasonable hours |\n| **No child labour** | Children are not used as workers |\n| **Environmental protection** | Farming methods must be sustainable and environmentally friendly |\n| **Community development** | Part of the fair trade premium goes to community projects (schools, healthcare, water) |\n\n### Fair Trade Products from South Africa\n\n- **Rooibos tea** — grown in the Cederberg mountains of the Western Cape\n- **Wine** — several SA wineries have fair trade certification\n- **Fruit** — citrus, avocados, and other fruits exported with fair trade labels\n- **Crafts** — handmade products sold through fair trade networks\n\n### Why Fair Trade Matters\n\n- Ordinary trade often favours **wealthy countries and large companies** at the expense of poor producers\n- Fair trade ensures that the people who grow food and make products receive a **living wage**\n- When you buy fair trade products, you support **better lives** for farmers and workers\n- Look for the **Fairtrade** label on products in South African shops'),
  fb(8, 'Fair trade ensures that producers in developing countries receive a ___ price for their goods. ___ tea from the Cederberg mountains is one of South Africa\'s fair trade products.',
    ['fair', 'Rooibos'],
    'Fair trade guarantees a minimum price that covers the costs of sustainable production and provides a premium for community development. Rooibos tea is unique to South Africa — it only grows naturally in the Cederberg region of the Western Cape — and several rooibos producers have fair trade certification.'),
  q(9, 'Why is fair trade important for developing countries?',
    ['It ensures producers receive fair prices, workers get decent wages, and communities benefit from development projects', 'It makes products cheaper for consumers', 'It only benefits wealthy countries', 'Fair trade is the same as free trade'], 0,
    'Fair trade addresses the imbalance in global trade where large companies and wealthy countries often profit at the expense of poor producers. By guaranteeing fair prices, banning child labour, and funding community projects, fair trade helps break the cycle of poverty for millions of farmers and workers in developing countries.'),
  t(10, '## Trade, Jobs, and the South African Economy\n\nTrade is a major driver of the South African economy, creating jobs and generating income.\n\n### How Trade Creates Jobs\n\n| Sector | Jobs Created |\n|--------|---------------|\n| **Mining** | Mining products for export employs hundreds of thousands |\n| **Manufacturing** | Car factories, food processing, steel production |\n| **Agriculture** | Fruit, wine, and sugar production for export |\n| **Transport** | Moving goods by road, rail, ship, and air |\n| **Ports** | Durban, Cape Town, Port Elizabeth, and Richards Bay handle millions of tons of cargo |\n| **Retail** | Shops selling imported goods |\n\n### The Port of Durban\n\nThe **Port of Durban** is the busiest port in Africa:\n- It handles about **60%** of South Africa\'s container traffic\n- It connects SA to trade routes across the Indian Ocean\n- Thousands of people work at the port and in related industries\n\n### Trade and Your Daily Life\n\nTrade affects you every day:\n- The **phone** you use was probably made in China or South Korea\n- The **petrol** in your family\'s car came from the Middle East\n- The **fruit** on your table may be exported to Europe or eaten locally\n- The **clothes** you wear may have been made in Bangladesh or Ethiopia\n\nUnderstanding trade helps you understand how the world is connected. What happens in a factory in China or an oil field in Saudi Arabia affects prices and jobs in South Africa. We live in a **globalised world**, and trade is the thread that connects us all.'),
];

// --- Lesson 2: Development and Global Inequality ---
blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson2 = [
  t(1, '## What Is Development?\n\n**Development** is the process of improving people\'s quality of life. It is not just about money — it includes health, education, freedom, and opportunity.\n\n### Measuring Development\n\nDifferent **indicators** (measures) help us compare development between countries:\n\n| Indicator | What It Measures | High Development | Low Development |\n|-----------|-----------------|-----------------|------------------|\n| **GDP per capita** | Average income per person | High (e.g., $40 000+) | Low (e.g., under $2 000) |\n| **Life expectancy** | Average lifespan | 75+ years | Under 60 years |\n| **Literacy rate** | % who can read and write | 99%+ | Under 60% |\n| **Infant mortality** | Baby deaths per 1 000 births | Under 5 | Over 50 |\n| **Access to clean water** | % with safe water | 99%+ | Under 50% |\n| **HDI (Human Development Index)** | Combined measure of health, education, income | 0.8+ (very high) | Under 0.5 (low) |\n\n### The Human Development Index (HDI)\n\nThe **HDI** was created by the United Nations to measure development more accurately than income alone. It combines:\n1. **Life expectancy** (health)\n2. **Years of schooling** (education)\n3. **Gross National Income per capita** (standard of living)\n\nCountries are ranked from 0 (lowest) to 1 (highest). South Africa\'s HDI is about **0.71** — classified as **high human development**, but one of the most unequal countries.'),
  q(2, 'Why is the Human Development Index (HDI) considered a better measure of development than GDP per capita alone?',
    ['Because it combines health, education, and income — not just money — giving a fuller picture of quality of life', 'Because it only measures income', 'Because it ignores education', 'Because rich countries always score low on the HDI'], 0,
    'GDP per capita only measures average income, which can be misleading — a country might have a high average income but terrible health and education systems. The HDI combines three dimensions: health (life expectancy), education (years of schooling), and income (GNI per capita), giving a much more complete picture of how well people actually live.'),
  t(3, '## Developed vs Developing Countries\n\nCountries are often classified based on their level of development.\n\n### Characteristics\n\n| Feature | Developed Countries | Developing Countries |\n|---------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n| **Income** | High GDP per capita | Lower GDP per capita |\n| **Industry** | Advanced technology, services-based | More agriculture, mining, basic manufacturing |\n| **Education** | Near-universal access, high quality | Limited access, variable quality |\n| **Healthcare** | Advanced hospitals, low infant mortality | Limited facilities, higher infant mortality |\n| **Infrastructure** | Good roads, electricity, internet | Often lacking, especially in rural areas |\n| **Life expectancy** | 75+ years | Often under 65 years |\n| **Examples** | Norway, Japan, Canada, Germany | Malawi, Mozambique, Haiti, Bangladesh |\n\n### Where Does South Africa Fit?\n\nSouth Africa is classified as an **upper-middle income** country. It has features of both:\n\n| Developed Features | Developing Features |\n|-------------------|---------------------|\n| Modern banking and finance | High unemployment (over 30%) |\n| World-class universities | Many poor schools, especially in rural areas |\n| Advanced healthcare (private) | Overcrowded public hospitals |\n| Good road and rail network | Informal settlements without basic services |\n| Strong Constitution and courts | High crime rate |\n\nSouth Africa\'s **inequality** (Gini coefficient of 0.63) is its biggest development challenge.'),
  fb(4, 'South Africa is classified as an ___-middle income country. Its biggest development challenge is extreme ___ between rich and poor.',
    ['upper', 'inequality'],
    'South Africa has a modern, industrialised economy (the largest in Africa), but extreme inequality means that wealth is concentrated among a small percentage of the population while millions live in poverty. The Gini coefficient of 0.63 makes it one of the most unequal countries in the world.'),
  t(5, '## Why Are Some Countries Less Developed?\n\nThe reasons why some countries are less developed than others are complex and interconnected.\n\n### Historical Factors\n\n| Factor | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Colonialism** | European powers exploited African, Asian, and American resources for centuries, taking wealth and leaving poverty |\n| **Slave trade** | Millions of people were taken from Africa, weakening economies and societies |\n| **Unequal trade** | Colonial powers set up trade systems that benefited themselves at the expense of colonies |\n| **Borders** | Colonial borders divided ethnic groups and combined rival groups, causing conflict |\n\n### Ongoing Factors\n\n| Factor | Explanation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Unfair trade rules** | Wealthy countries protect their own industries with subsidies while demanding open markets in poor countries |\n| **Debt** | Many developing countries owe huge debts to wealthy nations and banks |\n| **Conflict** | Wars destroy infrastructure, displace people, and divert money from development |\n| **Corruption** | Some leaders steal public money meant for development |\n| **Climate change** | Developing countries are most affected but least responsible |\n| **Brain drain** | Educated people leave developing countries for better opportunities abroad |\n\n### The Colonial Legacy in Africa\n\n- European powers colonised almost all of Africa in the late 1800s (**the Scramble for Africa**)\n- They extracted **raw materials** (gold, diamonds, copper, rubber) and sent them to Europe for manufacturing\n- When African countries gained independence (mostly 1960s), they inherited **weak economies, artificial borders**, and **divided societies**\n- This colonial legacy continues to affect development today'),
  q(6, 'How did colonialism contribute to underdevelopment in Africa?',
    ['European powers extracted resources, set up unfair trade systems, drew artificial borders, and left behind weak economies when countries gained independence', 'Colonialism helped Africa develop faster', 'Africa was never colonised', 'Colonialism only lasted a few years and had no lasting effects'], 0,
    'Colonialism lasted decades or centuries in most African countries. During this time, European powers extracted vast wealth (gold, diamonds, rubber, ivory), set up trade systems that sent raw materials to Europe while importing manufactured goods back, and drew borders that ignored ethnic and cultural realities. When countries gained independence, they inherited these structural disadvantages.'),
  t(7, '## Development Aid and International Organisations\n\n### What Is Development Aid?\n\n**Development aid** is money, resources, or expertise given by wealthy countries or organisations to help developing countries improve.\n\n### Types of Aid\n\n| Type | Description | Example |\n|------|-------------|----------|\n| **Bilateral aid** | Government to government | USA giving money to Mozambique for healthcare |\n| **Multilateral aid** | Through international organisations | The World Bank funding a water project in Mali |\n| **Emergency/humanitarian aid** | Help during disasters | Food and shelter after floods or earthquakes |\n| **NGO aid** | From non-governmental organisations | Save the Children building schools |\n\n### International Organisations\n\n| Organisation | Role |\n|-------------|------|\n| **United Nations (UN)** | Promotes peace, development, and human rights worldwide |\n| **World Bank** | Provides loans and grants for development projects |\n| **World Health Organization (WHO)** | Works to improve health worldwide |\n| **UNICEF** | Helps children around the world |\n| **African Union (AU)** | Promotes African unity and development |\n\n### Debates About Aid\n\n| For Aid | Against Aid |\n|---------|-------------|\n| Saves lives in emergencies | Can create **dependency** |\n| Funds schools and hospitals | Sometimes **stolen** by corrupt leaders |\n| Builds infrastructure | May come with **conditions** that benefit the donor |\n| Fights disease | Does not address **root causes** of poverty |'),
  fb(8, 'Development aid given from one government to another is called ___ aid. The United Nations promotes peace, development, and ___ rights worldwide.',
    ['bilateral', 'human'],
    'Bilateral aid flows directly between two governments — for example, Norway giving development funds to South Africa. The United Nations, founded in 1945, works to maintain international peace and security while promoting development and human rights through its many agencies and programmes.'),
  q(9, 'What is one argument against development aid?',
    ['It can create dependency and does not always address the root causes of poverty', 'It always works perfectly', 'Aid is never stolen or misused', 'Developing countries do not need any help'], 0,
    'While aid saves lives and funds essential services, critics argue that it can create dependency (countries relying on aid instead of building their own economies), be misused or stolen by corrupt officials, and come with conditions that benefit the donor country more than the recipient. Many experts argue that fair trade is more effective than aid in reducing poverty.'),
  t(10, '## The Sustainable Development Goals\n\nIn **2015**, all United Nations member countries adopted the **Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)** — 17 goals to be achieved by **2030**.\n\n### Key SDGs Relevant to Grade 6 Social Sciences\n\n| Goal | Target |\n|------|--------|\n| **Goal 1: No Poverty** | End poverty in all its forms everywhere |\n| **Goal 2: Zero Hunger** | End hunger and promote sustainable agriculture |\n| **Goal 4: Quality Education** | Ensure inclusive, quality education for all |\n| **Goal 6: Clean Water** | Ensure clean water and sanitation for all |\n| **Goal 10: Reduced Inequalities** | Reduce inequality within and between countries |\n| **Goal 13: Climate Action** | Take urgent action to combat climate change |\n| **Goal 16: Peace and Justice** | Promote peaceful societies and access to justice |\n\n### How South Africa Is Working Towards the SDGs\n\n- **No Poverty**: Social grants (child support grant, old age pension) reach millions of South Africans\n- **Quality Education**: Free schooling for the majority of learners; the National School Nutrition Programme feeds millions of children daily\n- **Clean Water**: The right to water is in the Constitution; water infrastructure is being built in rural areas\n- **Reduced Inequalities**: B-BBEE (Broad-Based Black Economic Empowerment) aims to address historical inequality\n\n### You and the SDGs\n\nThe SDGs are not just for governments — every person can contribute:\n- **Reduce waste** to protect the environment (Goal 13)\n- **Study hard** to get a good education (Goal 4)\n- **Save water** in a water-scarce country (Goal 6)\n- **Treat everyone with respect** regardless of background (Goal 10)\n\nThe SDGs remind us that development is a **shared responsibility** — the challenges we face are global, and the solutions must be too. As young South Africans, you have both the right and the responsibility to help build a better future for all.'),
];

